      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Select Committee that was appointed to con- sider the unemployed question have now finished their labours, atid their report will be laid before Parliament. In this connection it may be interest- ing to note that the British representative at D ussel- ldorf has just forwarded to Lord Salisbury an im- portant account of the measuresjwhich have been adopted in Cologne for dealing with thi complex social problem. It appears that in Cologne the au- thorities have established an insurance fund, which is controlled by popular management, by means of which the working classes are encouraged to pro- vide for themselves a regular allowance in cases where they are unfortunately thrown out of employment. The funds are provided by workmen's subscriptions, by voluntary contributions, and an annual grant of £4OO by the City Council. The benefits of the fund are confined to "workmen who are at least eighteen years of age and have had their doinicile in the community of Cologne for at least two years." Payment of a subscription of 3d. per week will insure a " daily allowance for the first twenty work-days on which no work can be obtained" of 29. for a married workman or a widower and Is. 6d. for an unmarried workman. For the rest of the time the daily allowance is fixed at one-half of these sums. The scheme is managed by eighteen representatives, consisting in equal numbers of employers and workmen. A labour registry has likewise been established, to which em- ployers and workmen are requested to apply.</p>
        <p>It is understood that on the conclusion of the sittings of the Pacific Cable Conference the Cana- dian delegates and Mr. Chamberlain will meet specially to discuss a plan for Imperial defence in which the Dominion of Canada might take a more iiberal share. Sir Charles Tupper, who Inas long in London as the Canadian High Comirissioner, has since his accession to Ministerial office in Ottawa given especial attention to the development of such a scheme, and the Secretary of State for the Colonies was acquainted with the details of i, so far as Canada itself is concerned, immediately oa the arrival here of the Canadian delegates to the Cable Conference.</p>
        <p>With regard to Mr. Chamberlain's suggestion that if the colonies came into conference with tise mother country some form of commercial arrange- ment could be made on the lines of a Zolverein, lam now able to state that a 'colonial conference next year in London is in a fair way to be arranged. The last colonial conference was in the Queen's Jubilee year, 1887, and next year will mark the Queen's occupancy of the English throne longer than any other previous Sovereign. This has caught on'" with the colonials.</p>
        <p>The decision come to at the meeting of the Unionists on Monday is the ,wildest and most im- becile proposal ever adopted 'by a powerful politi- cal party. his so humiliating, and contains such a confession of incapacity, that not even the eever- eat critic of the government ever credited them with the possibility of committing so gigantic a blunder. An adjournment from August till 'the 13th ofJanuary 'means simply, as Mr. John Burns bluntly but honestly puts it.tbat the Education Bill is dead. In January it will still be the same session of Parliament as that through which the House-is now plodding its weary•way,and it will cart, with it all the liabilities that are left undischarged at-the adjournment for the grouse shooting in August. There are the Supplementary Estimates te be passed before -the end of the year, the Appropria- tion Bill, and other business which can neither be dropped nor postponed. Themtnwst, time that Can be given to the 'Education Bill after the reassemb- ling in January is two montbs,and a portion of this period must be allocated to the progress of through the Nouse of Lords. If the Bill is not. therefore, completely extinguished by the present postponement, it will come out with very little of its provisons left except the 'four shilling grant.</p>
        <p>Mr. (loschenns a humourist is not a familiar spectacle, but 'he really was very amusing on Tues- day afternoon in his reply in the 'House of 'Com- mons to Mr. krnoldicorstet's protestagainst giving such cooing names as 'Dove, Sylvia, Fawn. and Cynthia to torpedo-boat destroyers carrying-feroei- ous armaments. .Mr. Closchett explained that not only was convenience consulted, but these were old service names around which naval traditions dins- tered. All the 'fiercer names, he said, were ex- hausted, but our annals do not show that ships with feminine names have fought Pus heroically than their sisters with less graceful appellations. The deeds of the Saucy Areth use, though called after a nymph, live conspicuously in naval story, and -the Sylph and the Cynthia have diKinguished them- selves. The bestowing of feminine names on ships, Mr. Goschen went on to tell a merry House, dates from the days when sailors spoke uf them as "fghP:4" .and though now-a-days the 'modern ship, like the new woman, has been unsoxed end adopted mascu- line attributes, she still retains her feminine name. Reminded by Mr. Ferster that the next names On I the list are the Polly. the -Fanny, and the Amelia. Mr. Goschen added that there were the Rose and the Dolly also looming in the future, but he did not find that naval officers objected to such names.</p>
        <p>The Hon. W. P. Reeves, who is to be the guest cff the Eighty Club at their next dinner on the 26th inst., is a man of many parts. Nlt yet 40 years of age, he has been barrister, journalist, and minister, and is now Agent-General for his colony, New Zea- land. As a Parliamentarian he is only five years old, having entered the New Zealand Parliament in 1891 as Member for Christchurch. He was quickly recognized as the leader of the Labour section of the Libersl party, and was given a seat in the Cabinet as Minister for Education. To him belongs in great part the honour of welding the Liberal and Labour sections into strong union,and establishing a Minis- try which has governed successfully for more than five years. During this time much uqeful legisla- tion has been carried, including the reform of taxa- tion, of the land system, of the Upper House, local option. women's suffrage, strict reg.-dation of the conditions of employment, a lifercharrt; Shipping and a Mines Act, and a statutory half-holiday for shop workers. The young bloods of the Eighty Club have evidently invited a guest from whom even older politicians might learn -much.</p>
        <p>It is 'being arranged that Li Hong Chang, during his visit here shall stay at the Chinese Embassy in Portland-place. He is accompanied by his two sons —one of whom used to be an attache at the Em- bassy—and they and half a dozen of the leading members of his suite would also he housed there Much, however, will depend on how long Li Hung Chang is robe here. It is taken for granted that he will be received by the Queen and rumour has it that preliminary communications have alread-y passed on questions which he proposes to discuss with Lord Salisbury.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. W. Russell is now engaged i,j drafting the report of the Select Committee which has been in- quiring into the scope and operation of the Adul!er- ation Acts, and itis expected that. he will he in a position to submit it for consideration towards the end ot next week. As decisions of the courts have shown, the Acts are very defective. and 'leave to the dishonest ample scope for the practice of fraud upon the public. One of the latest rulings of the Court of ,1111PeTl'A Bench in an adulteration erase that came befAre them proves that even the judges are lax in their view of what constitutes aglultera- tion ; and it is believed, therefore, that the report will contein eoine stringent suggestions for the pro- tection of the consumer.</p>
        <p>A number of farms in Essex were pnt up to auction in London this week. For one of them the upset price was £5 an acre, but, nobody ven- tured to make an offer. Another with some diffi- culty, brought .£51.2. an neve. The farms had the usual farmhouses and buildings upon them. Nearly as much is charged for e.:lotments in some districts; and unless the Essex villagers have all been starved out of their sorely-depressed -es.unty into the Lon- don slums, the capital might, under the allotment system, have been all regained in two or three years.</p>
        <p>It is now, pays a correspondent, an open secret that there is Whig: split in the Cahinet.and that:Mr. Chamberlain find Sir Michael Hicks Beach are the leaders of the dissentients.</p>
        <p>A CARD KNinc'N itE Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Prescription is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality- kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak• ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed sta.nsped nvelope to Jos. HOLMES' Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man eions, Bloomsbury Square, London, and get full particu'ars of this great specific tree of charge.. Mention wbether for male or fornale. Beware of fraudqlent imitators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JUNE 20, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. JUNE 20, 1896.</p>
        <p>goods store-keener in New York, to be brought into the Royal presence by the American Ambassador, while the wives and daughters ,of those British subjects engaged in stinp-keepiZg'(unlrss they occupy official pasitiom(,are not received at Court. An example of the absurdity of the regulation has just been sLown. As we all know Baroness Eckardstein was Miss Maple, the daughter of a Knight and a -member of Parliamenr. But he is also the head of a largr furnishing establishment, and con- sequently neither his wife or daughter could pay horn- age to the. -Queen. Now, hoe ever, the Baroness has, by her marriages changed her nationality, and has just been presented by Mr. Bayard, acting for the German Ambassador. . .</p>
        <p>_ This seas7n the Princess of Wales has gone more into stciety thin she has done since the death of the Duke of Clarence, end there is no doubt the approaching marri- age of Pr,mcess Maud has had an excellent efrect upon Her Royal Highness, and it has given her a great deal to do and think of. The Princess is very desirous that her daughter should leave home well provided for in the matter of clothes, and there is quite unusual activity at Marlbcrough House with the coming and going of mil- liners, dressmakers, etc. Miss Mabel St. Atabyn, whose engagement to Mr. Maireice Glyn, is just announced, is the youngest sur- viving thughter and one of thirteen children of Lord and Lady St. Levan. Lord Levan owns immense wealth, and St. Michael's Mount in Cornwall, the family sent, is one of the most picturesque cf English homes, beir,g, situated on a rock and to which access can only he had by boat. Mr. Glyn is a partner in the well-known •L„king firm, Messrs. Glyn and Co., of Broad-street, London. unlike France, small families are not the rule amongst the British " upper ten," for the average is about Lord kbergavenny has had ten children, the Duke of Argyle twelve, the Dowager Lady Dudley seven, her mother Lady Lousia Moncriefie sixteen, Lord Ellesmere eleven,Lord Inchigrun fourteen,Lord Leicester eighteen, Duke of Westminster fifteen,thel2th Lord Pre thirteen, and the late Deake of Abercorn thirteen. Cycling hclds forth prospects of alleviating the bore- dom which the somewhat banal amusements of an En- glish watering place is apt to engender. Already es- planad,a on the South Coast are rendered cheerful by cycling lessons, which are perhaps more blessed to the beholder than to the receiver. Even the kindly dusk— in which many of these lessons take place —is insufficient to veil the 'frantic wobbles of the beginner, whilst the abandon with which bicycle and maiden fall bodily into the arms (if the teacher would be pathetic were it not so ludicrous. As an entertkinment, even the ordinary sea- side nigger is not to be compared with a bicycle lesson. Apropor. TrincAs Christian has never joined the noble army of women bicyclists, but she is often to be seen in the vizinity of Balmoral on a tricyle. Her sons however, are first rate bicyclists and devoted to the "steel horse." The Dute and Duchess of Connaught and their children are all cycling enthusiasts. Princess Margaret and Prince Arthur are both skilful riders and often cause much amusement by exhibitions ot what can well be called fancy riding. A cry for " universal lights." comes from the cycling world, 'for it is a grievance that while lights must be carried on machines after dusk,the same obligation does not attach to all vehicles. To a certain extent this is a legitimate grievance and it wou:d be an excellent thing if all 'vehicles driven at a rate of over four miles an hour were obliged to carry lights,as are bicycles and tricycles. But, it must not be forgotten that there is a gre it dif- ference, so far as the general ,public is concerned ; be- tweeb a machine whizzing along at twelve or fifteen miles an hour on noiseless wheels,and a heavy farm van lumbering along at three miles an hour with a noise re- serribling a distant earthquake. The object of compell- ing cyclists to carry lights is, not to punish them, but to protect the public, for although cyclists do not seem to realise the fact, there are a few people still left in the world, who use their legs in the old fashioned way. 'lt is a melancholy fact that John Howard Payne, the author of" Home Sweet Home" was for years without a home, and used to hear his own song sung in cities when he had not a shilling to buy his next meal, or a place to lay hie head. John froward Payne was born in New York in 1792. He was a handsome and engaging lad, something of a Bohere.an. Pecuniary troubles made Wm turn to the stage for a living,and when only seven- teen he made his debdt on the American stage. Then he came to London, appeared at Drury-lane when twenty- one,and wrote a tragedy for Edmund Kean. Among his plays was "Clan, the Maid of Milan "which contained "4 Home Sweet Home." At the time it was produced Payne was starving in a Paris attic, and did not receive even the £2she asked for the play. The cloud, however, dark as it was, powiessed a silver lining for things brightened and in 1841. Payne was made American Con-</p>
        <p>•adi at Tunis. Efeadgear at Ascot was a positive drew' of lovliness. One large hat I espied worn by a pretty girl, was com- posed of the palest pink chiffon, and had a garland of cherry blossom round the crown, with one cluster of cherries drooping upois the hair at the back. Bonnets I noticed were all trimmed to give a wide appearance and were worn more forward on the head than they have been while they were all high at some particular angle. This gives an air of height, but it must be done with -judgment as it looks absurd when a tall aigrette of towers bends about and sways in a high wind. The funny little floating leather that now seems indispens- able to the fashionable toque looks like a fox's brush. It has a curious irrelevance te anything else in the costume or on the headgear and its raison d'etre would seem to be the jewelled clasp that fastens it in. A. toque en evidence at Ascot one day, built of pink rostie and further adorned with one ()Mese fox's brush feathers, reminded me of an Amazon of thirty years since in the days when 'John Leech depictedher. It is a fact, although not known to the world in general that many of the exquisite gowns that appear at Ascot are hired for the occasion. For instance a woman of limited means can go nowadays to a good dressmaker and c.ntract tn be supplied with several dresses for use during the week, at a charge in accord- ance with their magnificeryie. At the end of the week they are returned to thequilliner. Of course, should the garments be soiled beyond "fair wear and tear" addi- tion payment has to be:made. The gowns returned into stodk are never re-issued in exactly the same form. Some of them are altered and refurbished to do duty at Goodwood or elsewhere, hut the majority of them go to Paris whence the" models " originally came. would fain make a protest against the linen dust sheets which some women lay over their knees when driving just now. Something cool is certainty advisable this hot weather, but striped linen rather crumpled and edged only with its natural fringe is not decorative. Grey linen trimmed with the ley pattern in navy blue and lined with strong blue linen :to give it substance would be neat, not gaudy, elegant but not expensive, and I do not see why a little subdued and decorous embroidery sheilid not be permitted. An excellent plan whereby to exterminate flies is to 'finely ground half a teaspoonful of black pepper and mix it with double the quantity of brown sugar, and moisten the compound with cream or milk. The flies will sat greedily of the mixture and it will be their last meal, for the least taste of it is to a fly,"poison. Here is a delicious summer drink :—Peel two lemons very thin and boil the peelinthree pints of water for 15 minutes, add the lemon juice and boil closely covered anotherls minutes. strain and add 31bs. of loaf sugar and one ounce of citric acid.boil for five minutes. When cold bottle for use. Allow a tablespoonful to a tumbler of water.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>COD'S PII.LS cure constipation and liver ills, dis- tress in the si.o:nach or feel,ng of fulness afterf eating. They aid digestion and assimilation o food. move the bowels easily, and thus prevent and cure Biliousness, Torpid Liver, aud Constipation. They do not gripe or cause pain. Sold by chemists. Insist upon HooP'S. is, lid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, Hats are really wonderfnl in their diversity this spring, and there certainly is no excuse for a woman wearing unbecoming headgear under the impres- sion that she is thereby immolating herself on the altar of fashion. As I have told you so many times of late, everything that is becoming may also be taken as stylish; the unforgivable sin in sartorial matters is for a woman to appear in anything that does not suit her.</p>
        <p>Pretty and simple hats are of green straw, with a huge cluster of lilies and leaves in front, spread- ing to both sides, and a bow of wide ribbon, draped with tulle in the same, or a higher shade, at the back.</p>
        <p>A smart little shape in fine firm straw is 'turned up with a lining of black straw and garnished round its orown by a band of rich broad black rib- bon velvet, from the oentre of whiclz springs a smart blackcock-tail wing.</p>
        <p>A stylish dress which would be especially 'be- coming to a young matron is made of shot pink and peen, blue and f awn, or opal alpaca, with yoke, vest, revers of •silk, sewn with the new black 'lace braid, which, by the way, is now sold by the yard in black, white, and cream. The sleeve has •a pretty drooping puff from shoulder to elbow, which falls 'ever the outside of the arm .only, leaving the oontour of the inside visible. It is made on a well-cut, close-fitting lining, as are all other sleeves, large or small, save those of transparent material, which are sometimes unlined, to -show the arm through, these, however,.are only permissible tor evening wear. A lilac and green •alpaca could have yoke, vet, collar, • euffs, and 'ravers of lilac silk, braided with cream lace, and buttons of gold and irrideseent green'filigree. A pretty soft lace cravat would give an appropriate ;finish to the neck.</p>
        <p>A fashiorruble 'bodice is of cerise silk, covered with black lime. The zouave rases- be of cerise silk with cream guipure flowers and beads, with full sleeves andlisse and satin bows.</p>
        <p>There are always plenty of inquirers for the smart dressy type •of bodice made of woollen or fancy silk lend wool material, -and where some relief colour can be introduced without using expensive trimming. The vest can be of piece lace, or of 'light soft silk, putin full, and drawn in below the bust with straps of narrow velvet, fastened with fancy buttons. At the neck there is a shaped, fancy collar of beads and sequins, which are new made in all the fashionable colours. • At the back :there is a V yoke, to correspond with the vest and 'revers, very similarkin shape to those in front, go down to the point, and then fall .apart, leaving an opening like an inverted V. 'Where the cfigure is broad, the vest may be •narrower, and the revers startalose to the top of the shoulder-seam, and the bulk finished with three tabs, forming a collar. Mlle bodice fastens down the front, the vest and ivelvet straps all .being fixed &lt;to the lining, and zeaniring ,no after :adjustment.</p>
        <p>oecasionally one sees ,a smart child dvessed Ia white, with coloured ribbons, and stockings match- ing the ribbons, and these at once catch the eye, and are too conspicuous to be in good taste. Tiny children not yet promoted to stockings wear white, tan, or flesh-coloured socks of three-quarter length, but white stockings are rarely selected, even ;f the hat, frock, and gloves are entirely white. Mader- wear is always kept to all white, as with short frocks coloured petticoats are extremely unsightly. lo Petticoats skuld be finished with goffered frills, or• with flounces of embroidery. The advantage of the semi-transparent lawns and muslins is that they can be made up over linings of pale-coloured lawn, which are really as pretty as silk, and are, of course, very much less expensive, and this is a gm...at consideration where a number of frocks are needed, and plenty of variety is an absolute necessity nowadays. Besides the desire for plenty of chasge, all washing material:, need constantly ironing between their occasional visits to the cleaners, for they lose all their attractivepess if they are not in perfect con- dition. For morning wear, coats and skirts of drill or linen are exceedingly comfortable, and a variety of pretty colourings have been brought out in these materials, which will make them more popular than ever. A charming gown in blue pongee with blurred Chine designs of pale pink blossoms and green foliage, has a plain, fluted skirt, and sleeves which choice oieine csgureatrue ltaacbes, wathitchhe fwalrlisotvse, showing frills rthoewhand. of The he The bodice has a vest of accordion-pleated creamy spotted net, and over the shoulders is a kind of narrow flchu of the silk, edged with double rows of creamy lace. This shows a short pointed vest )f net at the back, and ends in points at the waist .o front, where it is fastened with china Pompadour buttons. The collar is folded and the waistband are both of blue satin, and tufts of creamy lace fall over the collar at the back of the neck.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>It appears that Her Majesty intends to overhaul the rules with regard to Drawing Rooms and make some im- portant alterations. For instance a chane is to be made in the new rule which was intended to put a limit on the number of " nobodies," who rush to these functions,but which has, as y;4, only succeeded in altogether ousting many who would seem to have an inherent right to at- tend. One rule we cannot but think needs alteration, vix., that which alloys the spouse and progeny of a dry</p>
