      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TERRIBLE SHIPPING DISASTER.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE SHIPPING DISASTER.</p>
        <p>A CAPE LINER SUNK. 244 PA.SbENGERS AND SAILOR:, LOST. ONLY THREE SAVED.</p>
        <p>A Lloyd's telegram from Ushant, timed 340 p.m. on Wednesday, conveyed the startling information that a steamer, supposed to be the Cape liner Drummond Castle, of London, had been sunk near Ushant. Further messages soon came to hand which placed it beyond doubt that the Drummond Castla had gone down, about midnight on Tuesday, with practically all on hoard,nnly three persons being saved out of a crew of 104,including officers, and 143 passengers. The Drummond Castle was on the intermediate service between Capetown and Lon- don. She was last reported from Las Palmas, which port she left the previous Friday afternoon for London direct. She was commanded by Captain W. W. Pierce, was built at Govan in 1881, and was the sister ship to the Garth Castle, her gross tonnage being 3,663. The first distinct information received by the company was contained in a telegram of which the following is a translation, from the principal lighthouse-keeper at Ushant :—" Drummond Castle wracked about midnight 1611, Ushant. Marquardt sole snrvivor at Ushant; two others saved are at the The de Molene, near Ushant. Six bodies recovered, including one officer of the ship,about 25 years of age, small light moustache, and young girl about six years old. The bodies placed in a special house. Kindly telegraph instructions for burial. The ship sank in three minutes." _</p>
        <p>Another telegram received at Lloyd's fram Ushant last evening stated :—Drummond Castle wrecked last midnight ; supposed on rocks. About three minutes after stranding the vessel sank. One man only, Mr. Marquardt saved. Another ship's boat was launched. Remainder supposed lost." From Mr. Marvardt, a survivor, the owners of the vessel on Thursday evening received the following message :—" Drummond Castle total loss off Ushant. Am probably sole survivor. Proceeding to London as soon as possibIe.—MARQOARDT." The owners have telegraphed to Capetown and Las Palmas for a complete list of the passengers on board. The latest cables advise 143 passengers on board, while the officers and crew numbered 104 in all.</p>
        <p>The first news of the disaster was received in London about four o'clock on Wednesday afternoon by an indi- rect route, and it was at once communicated to the officials of the company. At that time the company had not received a word from their agent on the spot, and for a time they refused to credit the news, although it was admitted that the Drummond Castle would he in the neighbourhood of the scene of the reported disaster at about the time when it was reported to have occurred. It was not till past five o'clock that the Company re- ceived a telegram, in the French language, from the lighthouse-keeper at Ushant giving full confirmation, and asking for instructions. Less than half an hour later came the telegram from Mr. Marquardt,thrilling in its simplicity, announcing the disaster, and stating that he was probably the sole survivor of the most terrible shipping catastrophe that has occurred since the North German liner went down in the North Sea with nearly :ill on board. This is the first occasion on ix hich the life of a passenger has been lost through shipwreck or accident in one of the Company's vessels. Numerous inquiries were made during the evening at the Com- pany's offices by persons having relatives and friends on board the vessel, but all were kept waiting while the official statement was being prepared. This was read to the people assembled in the office at half-past seven. When the awful nature of the intelligence was fully rea- lised a heartrending scene was witnessed. Many utterly bloke down, and the case of a poor sailor who bad lost his two brothers was ( specially distressing. The officials at that time were of opinion that,the vessel having Bunk in three minutes, a collision must have occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PLACE WIT a A WELSH</head>
        <p>THE PLACE WIT a A WELSH</p>
        <p>NAME.</p>
        <p>Te the ordinary Eaglish tongue the village of Gwynfa, Bynteg, in Wales, has a name not to be taken in vain. Nevertheless, important events have lately taken place there, as the Wrexham Advertiser shows. The writer obtained his information by calling at the house of Mr. Edward Hughes, there. He was received by Miss Sarah Ann Hughes, who told him a remarkable and interest- ing story. Mi s Hughes, who was in robust health, was formerly an invalid. She said she had been subject to heart disease for several years, and was unable to walk even a few yards without support. She had been constantly attended by medictl men, but deriving no permanent benefit, was sent to the Convalescent Home at Rhyl. Here she became so ill that she was sent home ; and it was clear that the only end of the case that could be anticipated was death. Her condition was one of ex- treme weakness and prostration, with severe heart complicatiuns. While in this sad condition, al neighbour seeing in the paper an account of the extraordinary cures effected by Dr. Williams Pink Nile for Pale People. suggested her trying them. Miss Hughes agreed to do so, and the result is probably unique. It was in the spring of 1894 that she commenced to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. She says that at the time she thought she was dying. The re-action was immediate, however: within one week from the first dose she was able to rise and dress and was in better health than she had ever been before ! Her heart was working naturally, and her walking p)wers were restored. So well did she keep, that she took no more pills, nor did she have medicine of any kind. Her life therefore has undoubtedly been saved by this remedy. Miss Hughes added that on the previous Sunday she had twice walked to Mount Zion Chipel, a place of worship at a considerable distance from the house,"and" she said " the piator rem irked (as it happened); that two years ago he had hardly expected that I would ever get to chapel again." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People owe their reputation to the wonderful cures they have affected in heart disease, palpitations, rheu- matism, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, sciatica, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anaemia, pale and sallow complexion, weakness, loss of appetite, pains in the back, female weakness, and hysteria. These Pills are genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d. They are a tonic—the only tonic pill—not a purgative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During last week, which was the 23rd, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington Latchford 1</p>
        <p>Total 4O</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 27. The death rate from all causes was equal to 116 per I,ooo,ac,minst 13 8 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 18 1 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 88 per :,000, agiinst 0.8 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against Nil the corresponding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- 0— 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 5 4 4 13</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from whoc,ping cough,- 1 from cancer, 2 from old age, 3 from heart disease, 2 from debility The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week i—Scarlet fever 1 case, diphtheria, 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital on June 6th, —6.</p>
        <p>METROROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 60 Fah., and therefore 6 5 degrees higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 75F., was recorded on the 4th inst., and the minimum temperature 37F., on the Ist inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall rneasured '99 inch. The general directinn was S.W. the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1000</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>The epidemic of ch.dera in Ec,ypt is causing heavy mortality. The Egyptian troops at K)rosko have been attacked by the pestilence.</p>
