      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CURVATURE IN CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CURVATURE IN CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>When baby is just beginning to toddl, about. you 01 ten say she is too heavy for her legs. Now, if y, u will only go at the sti"j -et from the other end, you will be nearer right. Baby is not too heavy for her legs, but the legs are too weak for baby. Consequently, is you are not careful, her bones will soon begin to bend, as they C.id in the following case : I think that I should be neglecting my duty very much in not giving a true account of the wonderful re- sult of Scott's Emulsion in the case of our little girl. About two years ago, my wife took her to the doctor's as she was very uneasy about her. Her wrists were very much enlarged, her shins were quite bent, and she used to waddle along like a duck. The doctor said it was a case of rickets, and advised my wife to take her to the hospital, which she did. The doctor there ordered her legs to be put in splints, and she was to wear them for two months : but I very soon discon- tinued them, and my wife said she would try Scott's Emulsion. It had cured our Maud of bronchitis. and we thought it would do this one good. So we started, and after she had taken about two or three bottles, we could see a wonderful change for the better. We con- tinued to give her Scott's Emulsion until she had taken eleven bottles. Her legs are as straight as an arrow, and she is as fine a child as you would wish to see. Anyone is quite welcome to come to the above address and see for themselves. I am never without Scott's Emulsion in my house. My children drink it like drink- ing milk they are that fond of it.—Yours truly, JOHN TARTE, The Woodbines, Washwood Heath, near Birm- ingham, January 21st, 1898." Children need nourishment for their growing bones as well as for the making of healthy flesh. Scott's Emulsion supplies both. It contains the purest Norwegian cod- liver oil, combined with the hypophosphites of lime and soda. Bath of these remedies are essential to children who do not make satisfactory progress in growth, and in no other form than Scott's mulsion can the most benefits from them be derived. This is bacause Scott's Emulsion is a scientific remedy, perfect in manipulation, and better adapted to the needs of the system. It is perfectly palatable, and does not derange digestion. When children show, by weakness, paleness, thinness, or other evidences, that they are not deriving sufficient nourishment from their food, Scott's Emulsion will quickly restore vitality; flesh and strength, stimulate their appetite, and make them:rosy and robust. It solidifies the bon's, enriches the blood, hod makes their growth strong and vigorous. On the wrapper of every genuine bottle of Scott's Emulsion, is the trade-mark of a fisherman carrying a big fish on his back. (17)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>The " Chronicle " says :—Et letter from Buluwayo, dated April 10, reaches us, in which the writer com- plains bitterly of the ruin which the Chartered Company has brought upon the settlers in Rhodesia by with- drawing the police for service in the Transvaal, and arming natives who at the first opportunity turned against the whites. These native police, he says, have headed the rebellion, drilled the raw levies of the Mata- bele,and taught them the new tactics which they have disphyed. "It is no use mincing matters," he writes. "No amount of explanation will bring back to life he poor fellows who have been ruthlessly murdered. No amount of plausible speeches by Mr. Rhodes will compensate us for the loss of all we had in the world,and the amount of excruciating anxiety we have undergone. The time has arrived when the people of Rhodesia will not tolerate the misgovernment of any Chartered Com- pany." The unfortunate writer, who has been ruined by the misgovernment of the country,complains bitterly of the normal administration of justice in Rhodesia. He states that the presiding magistrate of Buluwayo is a youth of 24, with no legal experience. "There is but one judge in the whole of Rhodesia, and since Dr. Jameson left on his mad expedition he has been carry- ing out the duties of administration. The High Court sits twice a year, sometimes once in nine months in Matabeleland." He complains, too, that preference of the most marked kind is shown to men who have served the Company in any capacity. They get all the plums in the way of concessions, and the ordinary settlers and prospectors have to shift as best they may. Finally. the writer expresses the earnest hope that England will set Rhodesia free from the Chartered Company.</p>
        <p>Lord Russell of Billowen, Lord Chief Justice of Eng- -lan4, will attend the forthcoming congress of United States lawyers at Sayatoga. and will deliver the inau- gural address on August 20. The subject of Lord Rus- sell's deliverance will be international law, and it is believed that it will he largely a discussion of the arbi- tration question. The Lord Chief Justice has shown a continuous interest in this overmastering problem, and his utterance will have special value as coming both from a great lawyer and a distinguished statesman.</p>
        <p>The Rouse of Commons chess match came to a close on Monday, the result teing an easy victory for the champions of the Opposition.</p>
        <p>The visit of British Blue-jackets to the Vatican has recalled the stay which the Pope himself made in Eng- land while he was Nuncio at Brussels in 1846, and it is remembered that he officiated in St. Mary's. Moor Acids, and in the Slrdinian Chapel in Lincoln's-inn-fields. But it is not no generally known that, after staying for a couple ot days with the Brazilian Minister, Leo XIII. lodged in a street running out of Piccadilly, the name of which he is now unable to recognise. One place, how- ever, he recollects—the drawing-room of Lady Palmers- ton's, to one of whose receptions at Cambridge House he was taken by the Austrian Ambassador of the day. Members of the Naval and Military Club, as Cambridge House now ie, may feel certain that no other club in London can boast of having had a Pope of Rome inside its walls.</p>
        <p>The past week was remarkable in the history of art in this cnuntry,for the exceptional prices paid for pictures by British artists of the eighteenth century. On Thurs- day, at Messrs. Fisher end Robinson'''. Romney's fine picture rePresenting the lovely Lady Elizabeth Spencer and her sister Viscountess rlifden, was knocked down to Mr. C. Wertheimer for 10,500 guineas ; and on &amp;gully Sir Joshua Reynolds's beautiful picture, the Hon. Miss Monckton fell to Mr. El ward de Sterne for 7,500 guineas. The first-mentioned picture fetched—with nne exception—the highest price ever paid in the auction room for a work by a British artist—theexception being the Lady Delme by Reynolds. which Mr. C. Wertheimer bought two years ago for 11,700 guineas. It is said that on the rresent occasion the Duke of Marlborough's agent ran Mr. Wertheinter very close in the race for the possession of the Romney—which the Duke and Duchess had seen during the week,and greatly desired for Blen- heim,where possibly.after allk, will eventually hang on the walls of those rooms in which doubtless its beautiful models often ph,yed as children.</p>
        <p>The unemployed rf Tl4vre, numbering upwards of 5,000 are sending a delegation to Paris bearing a petition to the Chamber of Deputies. They ask for an immediate grant from the State to keep them alive until they can find something. " Our sufferings,"they say, "seem due to the present policy of protection to the farmers. It is, therefore, fair that the latter should contribute to the relief we are in the painful necessity of asking." The petitioners ask for a revinian of the tariff.</p>
        <p>This is the tercentenary of the potato. In 1596 the first potato w,g planted in Eneland, in Holborn, about the time that S'r Walter Raleigh was nlanting the first Irish potato at l'ouehal, near Cork. For two centuries the potato continued as a botanical curiosity. When first eaten it Was a dulicacv, sometimes roasted and steeped in sick, or baked with marrow and spice, or preserved and candied. Parmentier developed the plant in France, Louis XVI. and Marie Antoinette wore the flowers as 6rnamenta. Frederick the Great had to force the Pont neranian farmers to plant potatoes by the fear of his soldiers.</p>
        <p>The art of ballooning ia steadily progressing, and the problem of verical navigation in the air may now be said to he solved. Mere ascent and descent in a balloon ought now to he assafe as in a tower hoist, and perhaps rather more fres from inconvenience than a travel round a "big wheel." Recent improvements have come from the developmPnt of the parachute. Last September Signor Copan% invented an enormous parachute which fitted over the top of the balloon and permitted of a gradual and easy dement after the bursting of the bag. At Brussels last Monday Capazza exhibited a contriv- ance for regulating the sped of ascent. It consista of an inverted parachute hung below the car, which can he nnened downwards in case of a too rapid ascent, and t'us made to act as a brake. Capazza can now, there- fore, regulate the speed of ascent and descent, and, according to the "Pall Mall Gazette," his demonstra- tion of the uses of his new double parachute on Monday was completely successful. The problem of horizonta, aerial navigation is of coure still unsolved, and until further progress is made in this direction ballooning can have little practical or scientific ntilit, unless Mr. Andree upsets calculations by reaching the North Pole in his balloon. Still the only serious danger the fully equipped aeronaut has now to fear is that of drowning.</p>
        <p>CA DRURY'S COCOA is both a stimulating and Tefresh- in, drink, and a nutritious food, probably more nourishing than any other beverage. It is absolutely pure, containing no foreign admixture.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL EXAMINATION REPORT.—The managers of the Church Schools have just received the report of the Is- spector who examined the children in drawing. The schools have nain been classed "Excellent." SPECIAL SERMONS.—On Sunday special sermons were preached in the Wesleyan Chapel, by Mr. Walter Jones, ot Doncaster, and collections made on behalf of the Wesleyan Methodist Local Preachers' Mutual Aid ASI9O- -</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS' TRIP.—On Saturday Messr.. T. H. Granger and Thomas S. Steel, who are the joint teachers of the fourth class of boys at the Church Sunday School, took their scholars to Frodsham, a few friends of the teachers accompanying them, making up a party of about 30 persons. The weather was delightfully fine, and the walk through the village and the climb up the hills were much enjoyed. Games were indulged in, and about five o'clock a refreshing tea was provided by Mrs. Par- ker, of Mersey View. Th, games were afterwards re- newed, and after the party had been photographed a move was made for the station, Warrington being reached about 8.30 p.m. PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A special meeting ot the Poulton-with-Fearnhead Parish Council was held in the schoolroom last evening week. In the absence of Mr. William Royston,the chair was occupied by Mr. Henry Thornton (vice-chairman), and there were also present—Messrs. William Houghton, J.P., George Mather, James Mason, Joseph Roberts. and H. Granger, as well as about half-a-dozen ratepayers. At the outset Mr. Mather stated that the meeting was irregular, not hb.ving been called at the request of the Chairman or two members of the Council.—The Clerk pointed out that the meeting was called at the request of Mr. Thornton and himself. Moreover,he had been in- structed to call a meeting after having received a de- finite reply from the Town Clerk of Warrington respect- ing the proposed Small-pox Hospital,and that he had re- ceived. He held that the meeting was in order. Mr. Thornton called attention to a fence on the south side of the Cheshire Lines Railway obstructing the pub- lic footpath leading from the workhouses, and moved that the clerk write to the owner or the said fence to re- move it.—Mr. Mason seconded.—Mr. Mather pointed out that the fence in question was not in their parish at all, but in that of Woolston-with-Martinscreoft.—Mr. Thornton altered his proposition as to read " That the Clerk be instructed to write to the Woolston Parish Councd asking them to take the matter into considera- tion," and on being put to the meeting Messrs. Thorn- ton, Mason, and Roberts voted in its favour. Mr. Granger read the next notice of resolution given by Mr. Thornton, which was as follows :—" That the question of repairs and work done by the roadmen both on and off the highroad euring last year be invest:gated and particulars as to matters pertaining to instruction, position, costs and payments be given."—Mr. Houghton I and Mr. Mather argued that this was a question for the I Highway Roard.—Mr. Thornton said he had anticipated ' thess objections, and he must say there was a little force behind them, but not sufficient to prevent him bringing the matter forward. There was d ssatisfaction. The public had a right to know; and the only body they could come to for the information was that Council. He thought the Council had been remiss in not investigat- ing the matter considering the dissatisfaction at the time.—Mr. Mather said it looked as if ti-e Council had done something which was not aboveboard. Every- thing, however, had been done aboveboard.—Mr. Granger thought they had no power to request the Highway Board to furnish them with the information. The Highway Board, he thought, would treat their request with contempt.—lt was anderstoAd that Mr. Thornton would see the Board's Surveyor, and if neces- sary bring the matter up again. The third proposition standing in the name of Mr. Thornton was. "That the overseers' books be thoroughly examined by the Council with the view of adjusting township assessments."—Mr. niason seconded, and a long discussion followed. A letter was read from Mr. J. Lyon Whittle in answer to the Council's inquiry as to whether it was the Cor- poration's intention to take patients through Padgate should Pear Tree Farm, Houghton, be turned into a. small-pox hospital in which he stated that no definite arrangements had yet been made. It was decided that no steps should be taken by the Council at present.</p>
