      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>TdE EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>TdE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JUNE 20th, 1596.</p>
        <p>IN his speech at the meeting of the itt:roMerley '4 cheater. National Reform Union in Man- chester on Wednesday, Mr. JOHN 14/I.ty dealt with the political situation in a Nh which will be thoroughly appreciated by Vin 'llaerals. There is something almost extraor- CrY in the state of political affairs at the present et-icle The Government which came into office !about twelve months ago, and which still has 410ritv of a hundred and fifty at its back,seems 1 to - e Practically in a state of collapse. So much th• h. Is the case that even Tory newspapers are 4141 ting at possible political disasters with a gene- to follow in the immediate future. (11'cline• to rumour there is already a split in the the Inet which, if persisted in, will at once reduce ,t) uionist party to a state of utter impotence. Whether there he any truth in these reports rltlrilnot it is quite certain that tile business of the fuisl'erinnent is beim:, bungled in an extraordinary ~,&amp;—'lcni• As a matter of fact the Gevernment is the country a kind of legislation which it dees g ee 11'4 Want, and which was never even hinted Prior t the 0 the general electio in. At Ll that election ao,i °ties Wen a wholesale victory n ancashi re, Lie,ll3tllroed fifty members as compared with eight liticeortv,4ll' Yet, as Mr. MORLEY points out, these were not secured by the promise of such 110.73 84 the Agricultural Rating Bill or as the be ill in the form that it has been laid thfe7-,e Parliament Several Tory candidates won this country in the cotton manufacturing districts loceog innntry by supporting bi-metallism and by larin favour of the revocation of the Indian "I,tte;il duties. Yet since then the most uncom- 7tte ising denunciations of bi-metallism have been e by the Tory CnarTenei.oß of the EXCHEQUER ‘°l4 the promises to repeal the Indian cotton r les have been entirely broken. In fact with tilajcl, to these duties Lancashire is now worse ofi nth.' I"as before. And what is to be said of the taeji4leasures by the promise of which working- P ipulous centres were induced to vote for eolv-uist candidates? Who ever hears anything Old frl:lta the party in office of such proposals as ipoir Pensions, or of schemes for providing hoth Ingram with the means of buying their own eae;:3 In the place of these seductive iited-411res we have the Agricultural Rating Bill , le E lucation Bill. Mr. MorteEY's searching aeadYBlB of the Rating Bill should be widely read Istildied. He pointed out that while the aver- cleif4te3 in the rural districts of the country are Leh"' 28. 4d. in the pound, the rates in several ticngelishire towns which he mentioned run from ° tiPon six shillings to over seven shillings in 13° untl. Yet under this Government Bill the I,:vitants of these towns are to be compelled to roundly speaking, at the rate of a It% Ing per head per annum in order to 41c)c1n the rural rates by one half. This iktrnstrok - la proposal, of which not oven the t:t outline was given before the general elec- Dro holds the first place on the Government's to ranume. The pretended object of the Bill is pf relriove agriculture, yet from the point of view lord° I"ing agricultural depression it is, as a land- told Mr. MORLEY "worth nothing:, the subject of the Education Bill, Mr. Monter tht more incisive than when dealing with fley'‘'lting Bill. "This hap-hazard, harum-scarum ett4iervinent thought it a very simple, one-clause beeL_quietiv and painleSsly to extinguish school tekenn but they have now discovered their mis- kill• It is now believed by many people that the vlitheaation Bill is as good as dead. It is to be the itlr4lvii and " hung up" for a few months, but %taotlnstility to the measure has increased, even tbtit4Pt the Unionist themselves, at such a rate itQt,o,it will soon be absolutely impossible to m- otile "nee it in anything like its present form. On snbjects Mr. MORLEY had some cogent 4eR:111148 to offer, namely, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S tii4Bti'd Customs Union with the colonies, and i 4 ell-Idati expedition. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S object a I,lpth'"lbt a laudable one—that of uniting the 1)14 mert .ountry and the Colonies in closer bonds, keth • DiotiLzv is clearly ot opinion that the Itic‘ ely(34._cls silegested for securing this end are more have an entirely opposite eflect. At the 1441 k time it is foreign trade which keeps the tc4itei:..!. English manufactories in motion, and 114Etito—::!us upon that trade would inevitably tteplo the throwing of many workmen out of Aetl4lYl3aent. That is certainly not the way to :=Not:riso the idea of Imperial federation in this ItYi• On the other hand there are several hie}, "iistries in one ca. ether of the Colonies Ivitticirlte nurtured by protection, and the sudden .4Nt„ see4,..wal of that protection which would be ;`411413."1 ill order to establish the Imperial Cus- 41btie • inn would ,for a time at all events,strangle thllleogaltildristries and rouse a strong resentment ahilnPortant sections of Colonial manufac- AZth-trl workmen. That, therefore, is clearly ti,°t• the cWay to strengthen the Ernpirel ludan expedition Mr. Mc:ataxy has Lit the moat unqualified condemnation.</p>
        <p>It is evident that in his opinion it may be described as an almost criminal enterprise. In the battle of last week more than a thousand men were killed, and Mr. MORLEY asks the Tories of Manchester or anywhere else to tell why they were killed. It can surely be no gratification to a civilised people to slay naked and half-armed savages by the thousand with machine guns and other arms of precision unless some high or necessary purpose is served by the slaughter. In this case no such reason can be given, and we be- lieve that Mr. MORLEY's protest against what is I neither more nor lass than needless butchery will evoke a powerful response in the country.</p>
        <p>The American THE nominations for the American Presidency. Presidency are always followed with interest; in this cour.try ; those for the coming elections will be awaited with special attention. Everyone knows that the Venezuela question has not yet been finally settled between this country and the States, and it is instructive to note what is being said on the other side of the Atlantic about the Manroe doc- trine during the heat of a contested election. SA far neither the Republican nor Democratic party appears to be disposed to give special pro- minence to the difficulties that have arisen with this country. President CLEVELAND, the author of the now historical .Nlssage, has certainly not improved his position, and it is doubtful whether he will be nominated again for the Presidency. The two questions which are evidently most to the front in this election are those of protection and bi-metallism.lt has been taken for granted that Mr. MCKINLEY would be nominated by the Republi- cans, and Mr. MCKINLEY'S name is, it is needless to say, linked with protection of the most rigorous kind. It is only natural therefore that the Republicans make the "protection of native industries" the first plank in their platform. It is declared that" The true Anserican policy is that which taxes fore ign pro- ducts encourages home industry, puts the burde n of revenue on foreign golds, secures the American market to American producers, upholds the American standard of wages for American workmen, puts the factory by the side of the farm, makes the American farmer less dependent upon foreign demands and prices, and diffuses general thrift." This is the high- flown language in which Republican wire-pullers describe the mythical blessings of a Protectionist policy. It used to be a saying of the great show- man, BARNUM, that the Americans liked to be humbugged,and it seems to be quite clear that the leaders of at least one of the great political parties are of that opinion. That the election is not to 1 be entirely fouglat on domestic issues is shown by the fact that the declaration of party faith that is to be submitted to the assembled delegates contains an endorsement of the Monroe &amp;crimp and a pronouncement in favour of recog- nising Cuba. Should Mr. MCKINLEY be elected and consider himself bound by his election pledges an armed conflict between the United States and Spain is almost bound to follow as a result of this Cuban resolution.</p>
        <p>In these days cyclists have a lot to contend against. They habitually grumble about bid roads, officious policemen and the lighting arrangements, while they often have to submit to discourteous treatment on the road. A case which may be included under bad treatment occurred to several Runcorn cyclists on Sunday night. The party was riding through As ton quietly, when they came up with a body of Irish farm labourers. Though inoffensive when sober the Irish labourer when drunk seems to feel full di " fi.fht" and the Run- corn cyclists unfortunately found that the body they met was composed chiefly of intoxicated man. The men refused to get out of the road and com- pelled the cyclists to dismount, and not content with this they added insult to injury by maltreat- ing two of the wheelmen. Luckily for the cyclists the men were arrested and at the Police Court on blonday a severe sentence was meted out to each of the two charged. It is to be hoped that the magis- trates' action will have the effect of patting downs nuisance which cyclists have complained of more than once.</p>
        <p>The subject of the vehicular bridge creeps in the minds of the public of Runcorn at regular intervals like the accounts of the sea serpent creep into the columns of daily papers with the dull or silly sea- son. There is no doubt the bridge so ardently contemplated would be of great service to the in- habitants of the immediate country in and around Runcorn and Viridnes,and the matter might be kept in the forafront of the work of the District Coun- cil with advantage to every ratepayer. It would be a considerable convenience to Cheshire agricultura- lists desiring to deal with the Lancashire markets, while most of the tradesmen in the town of Run- , corn would appreciate its advantages. The money for the erection of the new bridge would take a good deal of raisin, but to the authority—be it Pistrict or County Council—taking the responsi- bility of erection, the return in tolls woull no doubt justify the outlay.</p>
        <p>The grade Improvement Committee formed under the auspices of the Runcorn District Council have not been idle and a• considerable amount of quiet work is being done by it. There is in the near future more than a possibility that the Co-opera- tive Society will erect or take over premises to carry on the business of a tobacco manufactory. If this should come to pass it would open up a small fi-dd for female labour, which is a great want to Runcorn workpeople. With the exception of a few tradesmen's establishmants there is no opening for female labour in the town and a great question with Runcorn fathers is " what shall we do with our daughters?"</p>
        <p>The need of public baths in Runcorn has been shown during the week by the numbar of men and boys seen bathing in the canals at Runcorn. It seems a great mistake that the nearest bath—with the exception-of the canal—is at Widnes, but that everyone wanting a cool plunge has to first cross the bridge and then pay a tee to a rival Corpora- tion. The canals to novices are treacherous places to bathe in, and it is within the bounds of possibi- lity tnat further painful bsching fatalities will have to be recorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>HOMING NEWS.</head>
        <p>HOMING NEWS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S PLYINCi:CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club held its last race for Old Birds 'tom Jersey,a distance of 290 miles on Saturday, 13 members sending 54 birds which were liberated by Mr. John Buckley, the convoyer to the West Lancashire Federation, at 6.15 a.m. The weather bein;:,, so hat and sultry only a small percentage of the birds homed the same day. The first bird wired in belonged to Mr. George Richardson,Oliver- stre!A, Warringt9o, and arrived at 1.46 p.m. thus com- pleting the journey in 7hrs. 31min • a good performance considering the day. Result :—Richardson, 1141 ; Clare, 1107 ; Rarbould, 1091, Ditto 1091 ; *Richardson, 1095 ; z-Jarnes. 833. *Poo 3 birds. licaly should hold second position but is disqualified as also is Mr. R. Bolton. A MATCH FOR £5 ASIDE.</p>
        <p>The third match for £5 aside between Mr. H. J. Longton, of Earleatown, and Mr. S. Gibson, of Liver- pool, two well-known fa,neiers, was flown on Saturday from St. Helens to Jersey, and resulted in favour of Mr. Longton, with a velocity of 1,119 yards,against 1,052 by Mr. Gibson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURO Y, JUNE 20, 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURO Y, JUNE 20, 1896</p>
        <p>RUNCORS AND 'WIDNES.</p>
        <p>FREEMASONRY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the brethren of the Ellesmere Lodge of Freeinasons at Runcorn elected Bro. W. Upton as worthy mister, and Bro. W. R. Wass was again ap- pointed treasurer for the ensuing year. It was also de- cided to contribute £lO 103. to the Cheshire Masonic Benevolent Institution. MEItsEY MIsSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN. 0:1 Weluesday evening a meeting was held at the Sainen's Institute, Runcorn, and a committee was ap- pointed to make the arrangements for a grand nautical b..zaar winch will in all probability be held in N ,vernber. The obj: et of the bazaar is to wipe off a debr, hanging ov,r the InstitMe of abut £4OO, which was borrowed for the purpose of purcbasing the laud the Institute is built Upon. ST. EDVirAIIDi CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCoRN. On Sunday, June 28th, tne annual sermons in aid of St. J-4in Baptist &amp;moo', Weston, will be preached by Father Day, S.J., in the St. Edwards' Catholic Church, Runcorn. The subject selected by the preacher is in the morning " The Scandal of our Little Ones and in the evening "Of such is the Kingdom of Heaven." The timei, of the services will be found in our advertising columns. COLLISION ON THE SHIP CANAL NEAR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Oa Saturday afternoon just before three o'clock a col- Asion occurred on the Snip Canal between the tugboat, " Warrington," and the steacror " Percy," owned by Messrs. 13rundritt of Runcorn. The collision was I.i:it slight, though the" Warrington " was damaged amid- ships. The engineer of the tug was thrown overboard hut was rescued by Captain Joan finxworth of Percival- lane, hiving had a very narrow escape. POLICE PROMOTION.</p>
        <p>Well deserved p;;;;;-tiou has been bestowed upon Acting-Sergeant Benfield, of Sutton Weaver, who has been appointed to a lull sergeantcy at Northwich to re- lieve Sergeant Aleiander. Sergeant Benfield has been in the Runcorn division fat several years and is well known at Weston Point and Sutton Weaver. At the latter station he has been serving as acting-sergeant and his work will be taken over by Acting-Sergeant Richard- son, of the Macclesfield divisOn. RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY' COMMITTEE. The monthly meeting of this committee was held in the Council Chamber, Runcorn, on Tuesday eveninq. ?reseut—Mr. Wllandley (in the c'pair),Rev.J. Simpson, Messrs. S. Pope, T. Riley, W. Cunningham, and J. D. Jones (librarian). The cash statement for the month ' showed the receipts to be £5l. 03. 61., made up by £5O from the library rate, us. 8.1. in tines, and two other small items. The balance in hand is set down at £36 9s. 81. LOCAL CHANGES IN TES PRIMITIVE METHORDIST MINISTRY.</p>
        <p>In the report of the Primitive Methodist Conference held nu Friday at Liverpool, a list of stations for the coming year was pubil.hed. Therein it is shown that the Rev. T. Brama work in Runcorn will he taken over by the RV/ William Fidne. The Rev. W. Moore will still remain in the Preston Brook circuit. For the Warrington and Widnes district the ministers appointed are the Revs. C. F.nch and W. E. Lead. RUNCORN AND TRH EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The president and secretAry of the Runcorn, Widnes and District Branch of the National Union of Teachers —Messrs. Moulton and Crosby—travel to Landon to-day (Friday). Their object is to interview Sir J. T. Brunner and M. G. S. GiHiatt ; the local members in Parlia- ment, on several matters connected with the Education Bill. They will also discuss with the members the necessity of memoralising the leader of the [louse of Commons with a view of having introduced As early as possible a Bill to provide for the superannuation o' teachers.</p>
        <p>nig TRAUB QUTLOOK IN RUNCORN. It will be heard with general pleaiure that the United Alkali Company has decided to transfer the manufacture of dry soaps and blue from St. Helens to the Camden Soap Works at Ruscorn. The number of workpeople to be employed is not yet known, though there is no doubt that it will benefit the trade of the town to an ap- preciable extent. Yesterday ('Thursday) i the directors of the Company paid their annual visit of nspection to the worka at Runcorn and Weston Point, but whether this will have any bearing upon the extension of the i Companies works in the locality s not yet known. A NRW ROAD FOR Retie )RN AND WISSTON PoINT. During Mr. Ashton's negotiations on behalf of the Runcorn Rural District Council with the Ship Canal Company he mentioned the necessity: of a footpath to connect Runcorn with Weston Point. The company have since announced their intention of constructing a footpath four feet wide along their land which adjoins the Weston Canal. There have been a large number of drowning fatalities through people traversing the canal bank so that this dangerous walk being done away with will give general sdisfaction. COTTAmt HOSPITAL AT RUNCORN. A movement which, though practically in its infancy, is gathering force in Runcorn is that which has for its object the erection of a cottage hospital. The want of an institution of this character in the town has long been felt, and several influential gentlemen are making themselves interested in the matter. Though the movement is as yet practically in an embryo state there is every ground on which to predict that a sub- scripiton list will soon be opened. When this is done, there is no doubt, that the required amount will be forthcoming. Meanwhile, the Runc:rn Cycling Club will be the first in the field with a lantern parade, the proceeds ot which will be devoted to the use of the Cottage Hospital Fund.</p>
        <p>THE HEAT rAT RU,CORN.-DRATH FROM SUNSTROKE.</p>
        <p>Daring the week, particularly the earlier portion ol</p>
        <p>.• _ , it ; the weather at Runcorn, like everywhere else has been very hot and oppressive. Several cases of illnns have been traceable to the influence of the heat of the sun,while one case has ended fatally. Harold Outram, (igen seven years, a son of George Outran', of T, Curvm- street, was playing on Saturday near his home and the same day, overpowered, no doubt by the heat, he was taken ill. As the sufferer became worse en Sunday, Dr. Ormsby was called in but _despite that gentleman's atten-</p>
        <p>tion the child died on Sunday evening. Dr. Ormsby gave a certificate stating that death was due to sun- stroke, but the matter was imported to the coroner. Mr. Worsley has since intimated his intention not to hold an inquest. PROPOSED NEW INDUSTRY FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The varterly meeting of the Wholesale Co-operative Society was held at Manchester nn S-iturday when the questi-n of establishing a tobacco and cigar manufac- tory came up tor consideration, and it was unanimously decided that it should be undertaken. It was originally proposed that it should be erected in Newcastle, but upon the recommendation of the committee it was de- cicled to have it in the Manchester district. The com- mittee of the Runcorn Society having been made ac- quaiuted,with this, they prepared a petition, pointing out the advantages to be obtained by having it estab- lished in this locality. The memorialists showed that sites were to be obtained on the banks of the Bridge- water or Manchester Ship Canal, that the conveniences for water carriage were everything that could be desired, that Runcorn was centrally situated, and that the rates and taxes were satisfactory compared with many other towns. A deputation urged those views, and ultimately it was promised by the Chairman that the subject should receive the careful consideration of the committee. It is to be hoped that the proposals will be accepted, and that the recommendation of the Runcorn Society's re- presentatives will meet with the approval of those who have the -'ecision in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>II ALTON.</head>
        <p>II ALTON.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday even- ing, in the Girls' Schoolroom, Halton. There were pre- sent—Messrs. J. Stokes (chairman), J. Pritchard, J. B. Jeacack,ll. Hughes, W. McCulloch, W. Looker.A.Povey, J. Manning. J. Ackerley, J. Astbury, C. Birch, and A. Woodier (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TYE PINFOLD QUESTION.</head>
        <p>TYE PINFOLD QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A letter was rei:d- from the Duchy of Lancaster, with reference to the removal of tht, pound, saying *, corn- plain•:a have been made to this office, by the agent for Sir Richard.Brooke'sestato. of the injury, by trespass and otherwor, occasioned to his property by reason of the substitution by your Council of a flight of steps for the old pinfold of Halton, one of the fences of which is alleged to have protected Sir Richard Brooke's land at that point, and I arn to express the hope .of the Chan- cellor oi the Duchy that your Council will complete the improvement i which they had n view,by taking steps to obviate the inconvenience which is complained The Clerk added that he had received a reply from the editor of" The Councillor " saying " Counsel's opinion. The Parish Council are not bound to raise the wall which belongs to the ow tier of the adjoining land."—The Chair- man said that he had maintaiued all along that the wall I was private property,and they had no right to touch it. Mr. McCulloch nv-ived that considering the opinion re- ceived,the Council take ng further action in raising the wall.—Mr. Birch seconded the proposition.—The mern- bers,with the exuption of Mr. Jeac.ock, voted in favour of the proposition. A DANGEROUS PATEL The Clerk said he had written to Mr. Linaker about the dangerous pits near Halton Lodge. and had received a reply stating" I have to say that I have Hot been able satisfy myself as to the resp-insibility of the Railway Company in fencing off the pits you refer to. The foot- path, I telieve, when the railway was made was turned from its original course, and I am under the impression that they are bound to protect the public under these circumstances. If I find that this is not tio, I will have the fence put up as early as possible." A QUESTION AS TO ENCROACIDIENTS. Mr. Pritchard, alluding to the discussion that had taken place in regard to the pinfold, said he desired to ask if the land forming the garden enclosed by the wall in question was formerly waste land, and had been fenced in. If so by whose authority had this been done ? He went on to aay that in Cheshire, in that immediate district, large quantities of land had been encroachei upon.—Tne Chairman said he could not answer the question, and it would be well in future to give notice of such inquiries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEE riNG.</p>
        <p>The members of this club held their annual meeting en Monday evening in the large room ldjoming the cafe Bridge-street. Mr.Pnilip Speakman, J.P., C. Ad., occu- pied the chair, and amongst those present were Ms-rs. E. Ellison, C.C., A. Jordon, W. Carroll, J. E. Langshaw (searetary), C. Pownall (treasurer), J. Edison (assist- ant secretary) and A. Lightburn.</p>
        <p>THE REPORTS The Secretary (Mr. J. E Lengshaw) read his annual report in which it was pointed out that the last annual meeting was held on the 29th May last year. At that time they had their matches all arranged to proeeed another season uorler the English Union. 'Vie principal Lancashire and Yorkshire clubs, however, had held severe,' meetings with a view of forming what is now called the Northern Unien and after due consideration their committee decided to seek admittance to the new union,into which they were accepted. Their resignation followed on September 2nd to the English Union, the matter having received the sanction of a meeting held S pternber 13th. They were all aware of the formi- dable list of matches the team had to face and all being of a competitive nature made them very keen. As the season advanced the Runcorn team kept well together and being favoured with fine open weather, they were able to get through the season with the grand results which were already known to them. The first team played 43 matches, 25 being won, 8 drawn. 10 lost. The score totalled 33 coals, 54 tries, evening 320 points to their opponents 20 goals, 18 tries or 148 points. Throughout the whole season there were only 2 clubs who beat their team twice and took 4 points out of them. The players who scored tries were J.Faulkner 14, Myers 11, Robinson 9, Warder 5, Abrams 5, W. Pendle- bury 4, Butterworth 3, W. Gayter 3, Walker 3, Taylor, Farmer, Lightfoot, Y. Gayter, Dunbavin 2 each and West, Moore, A. Pendlebury and Mekson 1 each. Drop goals were credited to Warder 2, Butterworth 2, Myers and Pendlebury 1 each. Turner, Dunbavin and Hughes each kicked penalty goals and W. Faulkner seer, d a goal from a mark. It was very gratifying to the-n that the team which had shown such consistency throughout the season should crown all its efforts by bringing to Run- corn the new cup of the Lancashire and Cheshire League. 1 he team's success was eminently prpular, judging from the display both at Broughton and on arrival at Run- corn. The events of the season had proved that when the step was taken to j ilia the Northern Union it was a right one in every sense of the word. As to the prospects ref the coming season it was evident to members of the Union that another season as that just passed could not be very easily repeated and for next season's campaign another scheme was in force. It was proposed to have a Lancashire and Cheshire League and two Saturdays in March and all in April were to be devoted to a cup competition in which all the Northern Clubs could take a part, the remaining dates being of course filled up by friendly games. The cup ties would be on the" knock out ' principle, and th- matches would have to be arranged as the competition went on. There was no doubt if the players stood by the club in the same loyal fashion as in the big season just pessed,they would have no fear whatever, that the club would keep in the proud position in which they stood to-day. Tnere were sundry alterations in the ground which the new committee would have brought under their notice, such as the renewal of the fence at the Pennington's Rew end ,and a new turnstile and box at the bottom side. In conclusion the secretary hoped the members who were not players were doing what they were able to do to make some return to the players who had so 4strongly fought, and many times un- der great disadvantages, to place the club in the position they had. Speaking of the A team it had played 25 matches 15 of which were won 3 drawn and 7 lost. Runcorn scored 19 goals and 28 tries equalling 169 points to 8 goals 14 tries or 88 points. The scorers of tries were, T. Richardson 5, A. Clarke 3, Percival 3, J. Lightfoot, Gregory, Worrell, Shaw. Nixon, Richardson, Reece and Glytor, two each, C. Pendlebury, Wilkinson, Hughes, Urmson Johnson, Jolley, and C. Derbyshire one each. Goals were dropped by Jolley 2, Lightfoot 1. Penalty goals were kicked by Lightfoot, Gregory, and Dunbavin. The Treasurer, Mr. Pownall, submitted the statements of receipts and expenditure. ['he income was i-Sub- seriptione-31. players, £9 135.; 159 honorary members, £53 gs, 91. ; 48 stand members, £l3 45.: five season ticket holders. 174. ; gate receipts-stand £26 13s. 61. ; 35 matches, £667 6s. id. ; final tie, Oldham v. Runcorn, ' per Northern Union, £5O 7s. Id.; balance, Runcorn Cricket Club, season 1895, £8 17s. id.; Pares Banking Company, overdraft, £l4B 4s 71.; with other small items, showing a total of £979 17s. 91 The payments included-Pares Banking Company, overdraft. fieeeon 1894-5, £169 19 d. ; insurance of players, £B9 6e.; field exyrisre J 133. 111 ; rents, rates and taxes, £49 6s. 51, ecel and travelling expenses, £152 54. 21.; lost time, ee2 53. 61. ; refreshments, £73 3s. 5.1. ; foot- balls, jerseys, pants, boots, embrocation and doctors' fees, tlOO 7s. 1" • salaries, £22 19s. ; cash in Trea- surer's hands, . 3d.; and amounted to a total cf £979 17. i. 9.1. The balance account was- Parr's Banking Company, overdraft, £l4B 4s. 7.1.; balance in favour of club, £ll3 53. ; total, £261 103. 3d. By cash in Secretary's hands. £25; loan to cricket section, £25; cash in Treasurer's hands, £6 is. 31.; stand, £lO9 ss. 101.: footboards, rails, turnstiles, tents, and goal posts £42 is. 101. ; ad- ditions this season, £l5 ; loss 10 per cent. depreciation, IES 14a. 2d.-£5l ; pants, jerseys, boots, bags, bath fittings, Sec., estimated value, £44 15s. 61. ; total, £261 103. 3d. Continuing, Mr. Pownall explained that the subecriptien list was £6 less than last year,but since closing the books they had got in £2 or £3, and thee hoped shortly it woeld he up to the standard of 12 months ago. The gate receipts were £667 63. ld.,aeainst £458 4s. 51. a year ago, or an increase of £209, the largest amount they hen ever received. Triere was a alight increase of stand members. Time balance from the cricket climb was higher than last year. and they had reduced the balance at Paree Hmnk by £2l 71. 111. In the disbursements the insurance was about £5B more than in 1894-5. Up to Christmas the accident fund was in excellent form, but afterwards the amount grew. It was thought wise to look well after the men. (Hear, hear.) The hotel and travelling expsnsee were not so large, but the medical men's bills were £9 in advance of last season. On the year's workings they were £27 134. 10.1. better than last year, and they had spent fully £l.O on the ground to prepare it for the coining seasen. The Chairman moved the adoption of the report, and briefly remerked that the reports spoke very hopefully and told their own tale very well-Mr. Dutton seconded the resolution, which was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>VOTYS OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTYS OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in proposing a vote of thanks to the club players, said that as far as he was individually con- cerned, he would like to bear testimony to the very able manner the players in the football team had advanced football, not only in Cheshire but in Lancashire and Yorkshire. , It had given him the greatest pleasure, when he had the opportunity to see the team playing in different parts of Cheshire, and the secretary's report pointed to the success of their efforts. (Applause.) Mr. Imison seconded, and said no duty which should have been assigned to him could have given him greater pleasure than the one he had taken upon himself with- out being asked, that was to second the chairman's re- solution. He endorsed entirely the complimentary terms in which the president had spoLen of the team, and as a Runcorn lad hirnself—(applause)--he was proud of the success of the lads born in his own town who had done I so much to carry the football reputation of Runcorn into other shires. He was also pleased to know they had a reputation for gentlemanly play, and while there were times when members of the team were inclined to in- dulge in a little roughness, he thought the power to re- strain that feeling was something which they might be proud of. - -</p>
        <p>The vote having been passed with acclamation, Mr.W. Faulkner (captain of the team), in replying,thanked the propossr and seconder for the kind remarks they had made in speaking of the members of the team. They had all tried to do their best,and they could do so,as regards play, as well as anyone else. (Applause.) The Chairman proposed a vote of thanks to the com- mittee. and the Finance Committee, coupling with it the names of the Chairmen, Mr. Carroll and Mr. Lightburn. He could assure those present that tha work had been done efficiently and given every setisfaction. Consider- ing that there had bsen:considerahle depression in the trade of the town, the financial position was better than might have bion expected. Mr. Carroll and Mr. Lightburn, briefly acknow- ledged.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Mr. Speakman was again elected president, and in acknowledging the compliment he thanked them for the renewal of their confidence and hoped that in the future they would work as harmoniously as in the past. The following vice-presidents were re-elected : His Grace the Duke of Westminster, Sir John Brunner, M.P., Sir Richard Brooke, and Sir Joseph Verdin. The other officials elected without opposition were Mr. Pownall, treasurer; Mr. William Faulkner, captain of the first team; Mr. T. Warder, vice-captain; Mr. William Gregory, captain A team ; and Mr. E. Woodward, vice- captain ; auditors, Messr.. Lunt and Cooper. The voting for the Slcretaryship resulted as follows : —Mr. J. E. Langshaw 45, and Mr. J. Ellison 25. The latter gentleman was then eleeted as assisant secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ELECTION OF COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The voting for members ot the committee resulted in the election of the following :—Messrs. W. Jones. 61 ; W. Carroll, 69 ; S. Abrams, 58 ; A. Lightburn, 55 ; s. Houghton, 51. ; E. Key., 51 ; W. Collier, 48 ; T. Whin- yates, 47 ;J. Cragg, 45 ; W. Nield, 41 ; P. Deakin, 4C ; and J. Fraser, 37.</p>
        <p>PAYMENT OF JUNIOR CLUB FEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carroll called attention to a matter which had arisen but which had never previously come before them —the pAyment of the entrance fees of the junior clubs it-- to the Northern Union. Most of the senior zlubs were bringing in one or two for the purpose of strengthening , the Union. He moved that their committee pay to the Union the entrance fees of the Runwern Recreation and the Runcorn Victoria Clubs.—This was agreed to. Mr. Langshaw moved and Mr. Walter Jones seconded a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which was carried.</p>
        <p>FIXTIIIIES FOR 1896-97. FIRST TEAM. St,COICD TRAM.</p>
        <p>Sep. 5 Tyldesley H Tyldesley A 12 Swinton H Swinton A 19 Wigan A. Wigan H 25 Warrington H Warrington A Oat.</p>
        <p>3 Widnes A. Widnes 10 St. Helens Recreation ...A St. Helens Recreation H</p>
        <p>,7 Ches. V. Lan... ..Cheshire</p>
        <p>24 Morecambe A Morec tmbe H 31 St. Helens A St. Helens H Nov.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>7 Ches. v. Yorks Yorks.</head>
        <p>7 Ches. v. Yorks Yorks.</p>
        <p>14 Broughton Rangers A Broughton :Rangers 21 Stockport H btock port 28 Old liana A Oldham Dec. 5 Leigh H Leigh A 12 Salford</p>
        <p>19 Rochdale Hornets ll Rochdale Hornets A 25 26 Wigan H Wigan A Jan. 1 Legh A Legh. H 2 St Helens H St tlelens A. 9 T,Liesley A Tyldeeley H 16 Sa!lord 23 Broughton Rangers .....If Broughton Rangers A 39 Ro...,Lkiale Hornets A Rocndale H,Anets H Feb. 6 Swinton A Swinton H 13 Morse tmbe H Morecambe A ad Stockport A Stockport H 27 Oldham H 0 ldkam A Mar. 6 Widnes 13 Warringto" A Warrington H 29 Cup Tie 27 Cup Tie Apl. 3 Cup Tle 10 Cup Tie 16</p>
