      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>W ES TON.</head>
        <p>W ES TON.</p>
        <p>CHURCH BAND OF HOPE.—The members of this society had their first annual outing on Monday, Alvanley beiag elected for tha headquarters. A party nuinb•ring about 120 was conveyed in four waggonettes to Alvanley, wriere they proceeded to the field, lent by Mr. Wright, churchwarden. Tea was prepared by Mr. Harnamer of the Scheol House, who greatly helped towards the enjoyment of the visitors. The return journey was made in good time. The successful arrangements made were carried out and put in force by Miss Gregory, and Miss Matheson and the Rev. W. H. Stables.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>F RODS HAM.</head>
        <p>F RODS HAM.</p>
        <p>UNlow Cnnitcn ANNUAL SEamotts.—These annual ser- vices in connection with the Sunday school were held on Sinday. lhe preacher was the Rev. Joseph Johnson, of Ashton-upon-Mersey, who occupied the pulpit both at the morning and evening services. tk special service was also conducted by the preacher on Sunday afternoon. Special hymns were rendered by the scholars assisted by the choir. The offertories were in aid of the school funds and during the day reached over £ll. INTOXICATHD.-012 Monday before Messrs. C. Reynolds and A. Thomas, at the Frodsham Police Station, John Crogeu of Wigan, was charged with being drunk and dis)rderly in Main-street, Frodsham, on Saturday last. Sergeant Clays proved the case and prisoner was fined 103. and costs or 14 Jays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN AND THE MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND THE MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL RESUL T TO THE OPPOSITION. On Thursday, a conference was held in London, be tween representatives ef the Runcorn Urban and Rural District Councils, and of the Manchester authorities, on the subject of the Bill, asking for the confirmation of the order of the Local Government Board in relation to the Pert Sanitary Authority. Mr. Ashton represented the Rural District Council, and the urban Council was represented by the Chairman (Mr. J. W. Lightburn) and the clerk,(?dr. F. A. Lake). At the conference it was decided to withdraw the order with regard to the Man- chester Port Sanitary Authority from the Bill. The suc- cess of the opposition will no doubt be apprffiated by the ratepayers residing in the district governed by the two councils, for the relief gained to Runcorn is set down at something between I. and id. in the pound !on the rates. Since the promotion of the Bill the two councils have been strong objectors to their districts being included in the order so long as districts like War- rington, Widnes, and Irlam were left out and memorials were sent in opposition to the Bill confirming the order by both councils.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EMZLESTO w 3 ,Nsu Ar, WTON.</head>
        <p>EMZLESTO w 3 ,Nsu Ar, WTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Police Station, Earlestown, on Tuesday morning, Micha3l Callaghan was charged before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, with being drunk in Earles- town en Monday, and was fined ss. and costs; in default was committed to gaol for 14 days. GOOD TRIIPLAILY, EBENEZER LODGE. The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Hall, Earle-st. Bro. T. Dean, C.T., presided. After I several matters had been disposed of the programme I of the evening was given, namely, "Conundrums," Sister Owens responsible. All the members took part. CHILD KNOCKED DOWN BY A CYCLIST AT EABLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening about 6 o'clock, a boy named Richard Simms, aged 7, when playing with other children in Earle-street, was knocked down by a cyclist, but fortunately sustained no injuries beyond a few bruises. No blame could be attached to the cyclist, who with a party of others, was rid- ing very steadily. SUDDRN DEATH OF A. CHILD. The death of Henry Plant, aged 13 months,son of George Plant, 7, Alpine-street, Earlestown, was re- ported to the police. The child died suddenly on Sunday morning, but from the particulars for- warded to the coroner, that gentleman decided to dispense with an inquest, it being intimated that death was due to convulsions. SUNDAY MUSIC AT eARLESTOWN. On Sunday afternoon the Earlestown brass band was present on the football field, and played a selection of sacred music, to a fairly large audience. The following was the programme :--" E iwinster hymn," selections " Eureka," sacred song " Holy City," selection" Lucretia Borgia," chorus "Realms of Bliss,' air varia " Adele Fideles," and Hiawatha March."</p>
        <p>EA.RLRBrOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIRTY. The annual picnic given by the above society to its employes took place on Wednesday to Black- pool. The party numbering thirteen 14t, Estles- town by the 5.55 a.m. train,and on reaching Black- pool,breakfast was partaken of. The morning was very wet,and:much inconvenience was experienced. After dinner, however,it cleared up,ani a pleasanter time was spent, the various places et interest being visited. A start for home was made soon after seven o'clock,Earlestown being reached about nine.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY. The weekly session of the lope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke, C.T., presided. Two members were restored. A discussion took place on the advisability of hold- ing open air meetings on the Maiket ground, and arrangements were left in the hands of the business committee. An" at home" formed the programme for the evening, for which Bro. G. Clarke was res- ponsible. Refreshments were provided,and various games indulged in. BOWLING HANDICAP AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual handicap at bowls for Mr. E. T. Evans's prize took place on the Viaduct Institute Club green on Saturday afternoon. There was a large entry, and the `lowling throughout was very good, some close finishes taking place. After art exciting struggle, T. Compton defeated P. Talbot by two points, J. Mitchell securing third prize. This is the third year in succession that T. Compton has figured in the final, and has been the winner on the two last occasions. The prizes were distributed by Mr. Gee in the absence of the President, and a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Evans for the prize. NARROW Et3CA.PE FROM DROWNING AT EARL'ESTOWN. Owing to the very hot weather the Common pit has proved a source of attraction to numbers of Earlestown youths and boys. On Monday evening, as usual, there was a large number bathing in the water, when one, a youth named Thomas Alder, 17 years of age, living in Regent-et., was seized with cramp. He called for help, but his companions thought he was only playing, and it was not until he was seen to be drowning that any assistance was afforded. Another youth then dived under Alder, and getting him on his back swam to the side with him, and lie was pulled on to the bank. He was then in a very exhausted condition, and eonse time elapsed before he was brought round sufficiently to enable him to walk home with assistance.</p>
        <p>The Republican Convention of St. Louie on Thursday adopted by a large majority the " platform" which in- cludes a gold plank and a protective tariff on behalf of native products, also the Monroe doctrine in its moat extended sense. Mr. INl'Kinley was nominated for the presid Ency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>first fall in 7min. 4sec. Round two was different, the E iglish champion warming up to his work. He speedily showed himself the superior of the two and had both Matthews' ,houlders on the ground in 2:nin. 355ec4. The final round was alinost s short, though the American struggled hard for victory. Connors, however, was irre— sistible and threw his opponent for the second time, 3nsin. 29sec. The Englishman's victory was hailed with great satisfaction by the spectators. Mr. F.Turner, Warrington. refereed. After the last race Mrs. Thomas Morris presented Om priz-s to the successful competitors, who were all ap— plauded. This concluded a most enjoyable afternoon'a• athletic festival. . . .</p>
        <p>Messrs. E and A. Eustance supplied the prizes, which were a very handsome set.</p>
        <p>THE LINE OF FRONTAGE QUESTION</p>
        <p>AT NEWTON.</p>
