      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CLUB.</head>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SPORIS.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday was an ideal day for the holding. of the annual athletic festival in connection with the Warring- ton Rugby Football Club; there was brilliant sunshine, alight breeze, and a dry ground. The track for racing, on the Wilder,pool enclosure,was also in splendid condi- tion, indeed tar in excess of previous years. On some occasions recent rain has rendered the ground soft and the grass slippery with the result that the cyclists in turning corners ran more than ordinary danger from "spills." Saturday was remarkably free from such calamities only two individual riders being thrown, and then not with any serious result. On the field there were two tents one for the competitors and the other for the refreshment bar. The former was of considerable size, and necessarily so, for the entries for different events numbered 425. This large crowd of athletes hailed from all parts of the three countries and were in- deed a strong muster. In the tent where refreshments were to be had there was also a big crowd for the heat of the day combined with lusty cheering stimulated ' thirstiness to a high degree. There was likewise a good attendance, about 3,500 spectators being assembled on the field, all in expectation of seeing some good athletics. They were not disappointed either, for in every race, both on wheels and on font, there was witnessed a deter- mined struggle for supremacy on the part of some of the competitors. There were eight item on the programme and from half-past two to half-past seven the field was the scene of activity in connection with one or other of the events. The Warrington Borough Band was also in attendance and throughout the afternoon enlivened the proceedings with suitable music. A programme containing the following items was well rendered : march, selection, "Viva," W. Rimmer ; selection. " Eureka," Round ; valse, " Kiss of Love," Karl Kaps ; qnadrille, "Titania," Round ; selection' " Lucrezia' Borgia," Donizetti ; lancers, " Jolly Good Fellows," Williams ; selection, " Hulevr." Round ; mareh, " Avenger," Rimmer ; selection, " Lohengrin," Wagner; polka, " Entre Nous," Round; selection," Boulevardia,' Williams ; schottische, " Duchess," Warwick ; " God Save the Queen." Mt. T. Griffin conducted. The whole of the arrangements were in the hands of an efficient body of officials, and to them in a great measure be- longs the credit of the proceedings "going on" without a hitch. They were as follows :—Clerk of the Course, Mr. T. Bowers ; starter, Mr. E. Kane ; judges, Messrs. J. Evans, W. Lomas, and R. Andrews; committee, Messrs. T. Davies, W. Hewitt, G. Smith, H. Ashton, F. Barber, James T. Bate, J. Hackett, John T. Bate, J. T. Thorniler, W. Houghton, and J. Jones ; handic. pper, Mr. W. Platt; and the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. T. Pember- ton ; Hon. Seeretary,Mr. J. E. Warren ; Asst. [lon. Sec. Mr. S. W. Jeffery.</p>
        <p>FOOT EACFS. The first Pvent was the 100 yards handicap for which there were 66 entries, and eleven heats, the first in each running' in the second round. There were three prizes, viz :—First prize, 14 day marble cloek and bronze ornaments; second, fruit and cake stand ; third, timepiece.</p>
        <p>The winners of each heat in the first round were : Heat 1 : H. Hill, Ashton Recs., 91 yard. Heat : T. Harris, Barrow, 81 cards. Heat 3 : J. Pimlatt, Altrincham. 91 yards. Heat 4 : F. R. Mortimer, Hind- ley P.C., 10 yards. Heat 5: T. Horan, Warrington, 61 yards. Heat 6: G. Adams, Leigh AC., 10 yards. Heat 7 : M. Geary, West Cheshire Harriers, 10 ysrds. Heat 8: L. Wellings, Roby Harriers, 9t yards. Heat 9 : D. A. Massam, Thornton Harriers, 91 yards. Heat 10: J Shannon. Hindley F.C., scratch, and C. E. Heaton, Thornton H.. 9i yard dead heat. Heat 11: W. T. Reid, Hay deck, 10 yards. Second round, heat one, winners of heats one to six : 1. J. Pimlott ; 2, F. R.</p>
        <p>111ortimer. Heat 2 : Winners of ileats seven io eleven :</p>
        <p>,L. Wellinza ; 2, D. Et. Mass‘m. The final was warmly ;ontestad for the few selonds it lasted but Wellings.</p>
        <p>(Roby H )1 proved the:best man.'securint the first prize somewhat easily ; J. Pimintr, Altrincham was second ;</p>
        <p>. _ D. H. M,ssam, (Thornton H th;rd. 220 YARDS HANDICAP.—Another flat race attracting' large nnmher ef entries was the 220 yards handicap. No less than GO runners paid entrisnce fees for this event, which was made up of 5 heats, the first and second of which ran in the final. The prizes were :—First priz silver English lever watch and solid gold albert ; second, bread and fruit dish with bread knife ; third, flower stand. Heat 1 : 1, H. Hill, Ashton Recs,l6 yards; 2. W. Wellings, Roby H., 14 yards. Heat 2 : 1, .1. H. Barr, Roby H., 16 yards; 2, J. Rabbit, Warrineton, 16 yards. Heat 3: 1. 11, Taylor, Tnxteth A.F.C., 16 yards ; 2, J. Cunningham, Warrington, 12 yards. Heat 4 : 1, J. Shannon, Hindlev KC., 11 yards; 2, W. Walker. Stock- port, 15 yards. Heat 5:1. T. Harris, Barrow. 14 yards; 2, T. Horan, Warrington, 11 yards. A gond rare forth" final nlaees was again witnesst,d, hut the local men were unable to command success. W. Walker came first ; D.</p>
        <p>Taylor, second ; J. H. Barr, third. 880 YARDS HANDICAP.—Thirty-four entrants were down for the 880 yards handicap, in nomnetition for first priz., marble clock with ormolu mounts ; second. ca eof fish and dessert knives with ivory handles ; third, six-bottle cruet frame. All ran in one heat, and there were eight local runners taking part. Of these, W. Price, Orford Harries, was thought to have a good chance, and his running was witnessed with interest. Be did well, as will be seen :—A. Looney, Liverpool H, 49 yards, came in first ; W. Price. Orford fl. 53 yards, second ; J. W Clihborn, Widnes, 58 yards. third. ONE MILE BANDICAP.—The last of the fiat races was the one mile handicap containing 31 entries. The priv!s were : heavy solid gold hall marked albert ; second. engraved silver plated waiter ; third, hot water , jug. There were several local competitors including W. Price.Orford, and his brother James Price. Both were ln, ke(l upon favourably for the distance and James Price ran exceedinglyi well, securing the first privr wi h 125 yds. Fl. Looney, Liverpool, 132yds., won the second ; and J. W.Clibborn, Widnes, 135cds., third. CYCLE RACES.</p>
        <p>There were three cycle races down on the programme and each was largely entered for. Riding of a speedy character was witnessed in all, and local successes were fairly prevalent. ONE MILE NOVICE BICYCLE HANDICAP.—The first cycle rare of the afternoon was the one mile for novices, that is for riders who had never won a first prize. Nine heats, including 75 entries, were run off, and there was a good majerity of the riders entered actually taking part. The prizes were : First prize, silver plated tea set ; second, salad bowl and servers ; third, case tea spoons and sugar tongs. The first man in each beat rode in the final. Heat 1. :T. Harvey, Lrigh AC., 60 cards. Heat 2 : W. A. Crouch, Liverpool !Wheelers. 25 yards. Heat 3: L. Campkin. Liverpool, 75 yards. Heat 4 : F. 110-k, Crumnsall C.C., 55 yards. Heat 5: J. W. Tay- lor. Oldham, 55 yards. Heat 6 : H. Phopwood, Oldham R.C.C.,50 yards. Heat :J. Birchall, Wrgan W.C.C., 50 yards. Heat 8: W. G. Brown, Greenheys Vic. C.C., 40 yards. Heat 9 W. Collin, Warrington C.C.. 50 yards. The final Was a most exciting one, hut ere three out of the five lens were complete, it was pretty evident that Walter Collin was going to be the first man in. This proved to he the case, and Collin won handsomely, and was loudly cheered. T. Harvey secured the second prize and L. Ca.mpkin the third. ONE MILE OPEN BANDICAP.—Tha oaf, mile open handi- cap was also a popular event and drew 83 entries. These were divided into nine heats, the first of each entering in the final. Some well-known riders were down for the event, including several local men. The privet were first prize.revolving breakfast dish; secnnd.spirit kettle; third, toast rack and butter dish. Heat 1: A.. Hall, Sale C.C.C., 150 yards. Heat 2: S. A. Riley, Longsieht 8.C., 170 yards. Heat 3: J. Seward, Skerton C.C., 140 yards. Heat 4: G. Gurley. Manchester Star W.. 175 yards. Heat 5: G. H. Smith. Warrington c.c., 190 yards. Heat :H. Greenough, Wigan W.C.C., 125 yards. Heat 7: J. Parker, Mare. 175 yards. Heat 8: W. Collin, War- rington C C.. 175 yards. Heat 9: 0. Rears, (15 years), Manelester AC., 200 yards. 0. Reevy who was down on the programme as 15 years of age aroused e,onsiderable interest among the spectators, many of whom expressed strong doubts as to that being his right age. He was strongly built and looked at least five years older. Not only that hut he rode better than many of the other competitors, ultima`ely securing third prize. The final of this race rno,was keenly contested and up to the last caw varda it was most uncertain who would win. G. H. Smith (Warrington) got in first, A. Hall secnnd, and 0. Reavc third. The result was a popular one with the home spectators. Collin, who won his heat easily, gave up in the final. Two MILES HANDICAP.—Riders of high standing' flocked in for the two miles handicap, and speculation was very rife as to results There were 65 entries and six heats,the first and second in each riding in the final. A handsome set of prizes was competed for in this event, viz :—First prize, tea set with real Crown Derby china cups and saucers in case complete ; second, tea urn ; third, plated four cup egg frame. Heat 1:J. P. White, St.Helens C C ,165 cards.l; H.Greenough,Wiean W.C.C. 190 vards,2. Heat 2 : A.T.Daintith,Warringtnn C.C.,160 cards.l ; B. Newcombe, Warrineton C.C., 120 cards,2. Heat, 3 • A Hall, Sale C.C.C., 225 yards. 1 ; J. Dootson, Horwich. 173 yards, 2 Heat 4 : J. H. Scott, Wigan 235 cards, 1 ; G. H. Smith. Warrington, 285 reeds. 2. -Heat 5: W. W. Jarvis, Liverpool W.. 220 yards, 1 ; 0. Reavc, Manchester A.G., 325 yards, 2. Heat 6: r. Astill, Warriegton C.C.. scratch, 1; A. E. Maccock, Greenheya Vic. C.C., /65 yards. There are ten laps of the field to two miles and the race was therefore interesting. All the local riders rode pluckily and in the final, notwithstanding their heavy handinap.it was plain that Newcomt e and Astill would he at the top. Di4tancing all the rest these two kept together and eventually amidst great excitement New- combe wen by about half a wheel, Asti!l coming second, and A. E. Maycock third.</p>
        <p>A popular event of the afternoon was the wrestling match between Tom Connors,charnpion of England and S Matthews. champion of America. The contest took place on mats in the centte of the field. Of the two. Matthews was much the heavier but did not appear to he in as fit condition as his opponent. There were three rounds,at intervals,and the victory rested NO, h the man who secured two falls out of the three. The style was catch-as-catch-can and some very pretty wrestling was witnessed. In the first ro and Matthews secured the I The handicap in the 'above two races reprcsentedd I A. Macierson at Scratch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ENFORCEMENT OF A PENALTY.</head>
        <p>ENFORCEMENT OF A PENALTY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day before Mr. John White, Mr. W. Houghton, and Mr. J. Tinsley, Mr. Brierley,surveyor to the Newton District. Council, claimed from Mr. Garrett Ronayne the sum of £4, which was forfeited by an order of the justices at 20s.a day,and also the costs of the order 11s.6.1.,making a total of £4 ].la. ed. Mr. Carruthers, solicitor, Liverpool, on behalf of Mr. Ronayne, applied for a tortnignt's adjournment. They intended placing the matter before counsel with the object of opposing it. Mr. Worsiey, for the Newton Council, opposed the ap- plication. He said the case first came on on the 15th April, and afterwards on the 6:,h May it was gone into, and an order made by the Bench which had never been enforced. What they asked for now was the enforce- ment ef that order which imposed a penalty of £1 a day,commencing on the 6th May. Mr. Wordley said he took the earliest opportunity of bringing the case before the Bench to prevent the penalty accumulating too much. If it was adjourned until the next court day there would be £l4 penalty in addition, against Mr. Ronayne. The Clerk suggested that Mr. Ronayne should deposit £l4 and the £4 already accrued, in the court. Mr. Carruthers put this to his client who said "4 will not deposit a pound." (Pointing to Mr. Brierley) " That gentleman perjured himself deliberately. There have been no byelaws. There is not a particle of truth in the whole charge that was made." The Bench disallowed any proposed adjournment and made an order for the enforcement of the penalties.</p>
