      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BEFORE TEIE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE TEIE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON POLICEMAN SAVAGELY ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday morn- ing before the Mayor and Mr. J. H. Fleming, a rough- looking man named Albert Forster, 4 Heath-lane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Gaskell'e- yard on Sunday morning ; he was further charged with assaulting P.C. Jones at the same time and placo.— Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said he was sorry to say this was rather a bad case, and if the bench believed the evidence he (the Chief Constable) would bring forward he should ask that Forster be dealt with in such a manner as to be an example to men of his class. If this class could get a solitary policeman in a T.°, gh neighbourhood on a Saturday night they would assault him in a most savage manner. It ap- peared that P.C. Jones, who is an old and respected offieer,was called to a disturbance in a yard off Go!borne- street on Sunday morning,when everything should have been quiet,namely, between twelve and one o'clock. The prisoner was amongst those present, and the offieer en- deavoured to clear the yard and to get the people into their houses. He asked the prisoner to go inside, when the latter,without any 'provocation, struck the officer in the face. The officer then got hold of ties prisoner, but as soon as he h d done so—and this seemed to have been done by a prrconcerted arrangement—the officer was " rushed " into a houee wed the door closed upon him. While in the 'mule he was beaten and kicked in a most abominable manner. Two young men, altogether un- connected with the notice force, came to the officer's as- sistance and rescued him in an exhausted condition. After the assault the officer was thrown into the yard minus his helmet. One or the young men went for another policeman,and the result was that the prisoner was apprehended and locked up. He was sorry to say that a number of men and women attacked the officer, and in the darkness and contusion which prevailed he was unable to identify the whole of them. If evidence could be subsequently broueht a gainet -other offenders action would be taken by the police in due course. The bench would see the marks on the officer's face.— P.C. Jones, sohnse face was cut in several places and his right eve-blackened, corroborated the statement of the Chief Conetable, and said that he lost a great quantity of blood owing to the assault upon him.—The Chief Con- stable: I have had him examined this morning, and there are a large number of bruises on his body.—ln answer to the Mayor, Jones said he was s.sseulted by women as well as by men. He could not identify all the men, but he could swear to an assault by a man named Jackson.—A man named Bridge stepped into the box, and said the evidence which had been given was cor- rect. He assisted Jones, who was being kicked while on the door. He was thrown out of the house minus his kelmet.—The Mayor: It was a very strange thing to carry a man into a house and assault him.—The Chief Constable: It hal all the appearance of having been preconcerted.—Another young man named Jones, who also assisted the police, having given evidence, the Mayor and Mr. Fleming said they were much obliged to the men who eseieted the police, and they ordered that each be paid 5.. for hie services.—Prisoner said he was drunk and did not remember anything about the as- eault.—The Mayer: We are determined to put a stop to this kind of thing. We must protect the police, and the prisoner will be sent to gaol for three months with bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>7RlDlY.—Before Al4erman Harrison (in the chair), Aiderman</p>
        <p>Weigh+, and Dr. Darnall.</p>
        <p>REFRESHMENTS AT -THE CRICFCF:T MATCHES.—Mr. Wright. of the Royal Court Hotel was granted an occasional license to sell at the cricket matches during July and August. A RECENT CRUELTY CASE.—Tnspector Bilson. said that in the case of John Dutton and Sarah Dutton who were a fortnight ago summoned for cruelly working a horse, the animal had since been properly rested and well treated. He would therefore withdraw the charge on payment of costs.—This course was allowed. DOTS SENT TO THE " CLlO."—John Waddington. cycle agent. appeared h.fore the Bench to express his willingness to have his boy Clement, sent to the" Clio." as suggested by the School Attendance Committee.— The Chief Constable said the boy was unmanageab e, and he knew that Mr. Wadding- ton had done his best to control him.—The boy who is '0 years of age was sent to the "CH," until he was 16 years of age.--Tohn Cartledge. son of Joseph Cartledge 3. Ryland, yard, Foundry-st., was also committed to the " Clio " until 16 years of age. as an incorrieible. THE INTERFAMILY c'QUARBLE: FURTHER DEVELOPMENTS. —Cornelius Ward, Bakehouse-yard. was summoned for sissaulting kis sister-in-law, Mary Ward, Buckley's yard.— Mrs. Ward said that as a result of the case last week, dsfend- ant and his mother threatened her and eventutily he struck her on the head and knocked her down.—Hvidence was given on both sides.—lnspector Taylor said he saw the parties after the row and complainant then did not mention any assault. Defendant teemed to he the complainant as he said they were laying in wait for him.—The bench bound defendant over to keep the peace for 6 months and ordered him to pay the costs, 10t. A GIRL OF TEN AS COMPLAINANT.—EIizabath Kelly, 43- Sharp-street. a girl, was charged with assaulting Catherine Cogan, aged 10. on the 9th June.—Complainant said defend- ant had laid on her hecause a little girl she had with her touched an umbrella that a child who awes with defendant had.—Defendant said that complainant tore a paper umbrella her little sister had. She " tapped " her on the shoulder for it.—The case was dismistel.</p>
        <p>SISTER-1N- LAWS AT V.suliNCF:.—Alice Stacy, 22 Dickenson- st., was charged with assaulting Lily Stacy, 40 liattershy- lane. Complainant, who is defendant's sister-in-law, said the latter struck her and pulled her hair because she had, she said, made allegations ah.ut her.—The case was dismissed being "six of one and half-a-dozen of the other."</p>
        <p>SITURDAY.—Before Mr. Burgess (in the chair), Mr. I. W Hayward, and Mr. S. Wright. HE HAD COME DOWN IN THE WORLD.—Charies Pink was eharged with being drunk and disorderly' in Butterrnarket-st. on the 12th inst.—lnspector Spinks stated that he was going through Buttormarket-st. OR the above date and saw the de- fendant, wht appeared to be drunk. He was going through the sword exercise. He persuaded him to desist, and then took him to the police station. There was no doubt that the prisoner was drunk.—. Defendant: I want a definition of what you mean by "drunk."—lnspector Spinks : I could see that you were nervous, nnd spoke incoherently. You were going through the sword exercise—what no sober man would do in the street.—Defendant: I dd not know thet I was doing wrong. I was only swinging a stic c. I alwevs do that. I was simply walking through the town.—The Chief Con- stable stated that the defendant had formerly held a high position, haying been a medical student, and he had also been connected with an opera company. He was a little drunk and seemed temporarily means The defendant seemed better now. The magistrates discharged the prisoner On his promise to leave the town.—Defendant: Thank you. Pair.mto TO MAINTAIN • WlVE.—Richard Haddock was charged by the Warrington Union with failing to maintain his wife, and leaving her charge tble to that union.—Mr. Shearer, relieving and enquiry officer, who prosecuted, stated that defendant's wife was admitted into the union on the 14th May. He was apprehended on Friday night, when leav- ing a lodging-house. The children were chargeable to the union to the amount of £1 a week.—The defendant stated CARTER'S LITTLE</p>
