        <p>that he was about to make inquiries about his wife and chil- dren. —The case was adjourned tor four weeks on the promise th-tt the defendant would remove his children from the house _ _</p>
        <p>A WANTON PASTIME. ENORMOUS DAMAGE TO GAS LAMPS —Hobert Riley iind James Steel, two youths, were charged with breaking panes of glass in gas lamps on the morning of June 13th, in Orford-avenue. —lietective Barlow stated that on the morning of the 13th Junn,having received information th t sever 1 gas lamps had been injured, he and another officer secreted themselves in the street and saw the defen- dants break the glass. Recently there had been £5 worth of lamp glasses broker —Mr. F. Taylor, secretary of the Gas Comp ny stated that damage to th • mount of .26) and £7) had been d me. Within the last fortnight ten squares had been broken in the saws avenue. That district was the worst In the neighbourhoo for this kind of damage. Several lamp glasses hal been dunolished wholesale He was sure they lia.l been wilfully damaged. They had pieces of brick and iron which were trequently thrown at the lamps. The Iron came from the forge.—The Bench fined the lads ss. and cots, or in default seven days imorisonrnent. The Chairiran cau- tioned the youths, at,d said they wonld be severely dealt with if they came there again.</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before he. Mayor,_ Mr. J. If. Fleming, and</p>
        <p>A dernian 'Sutton</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DIS',RDERLF.—Jane Mather, Leonard-street, was charged with ha ,ing been drunk and disorderly on Saturday night in Dial-st. She was in a helpless condition. Fined ss. and costs. -Peter C'orrboy, 18, Peroival's-fold was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Butter- market-st. at had-past nine on Sunday evening. Defendant was discharged with a caution.- Thomas Bate, labourer, re- st PLinees-brow, was charged with having been drunk and disord;rlv in Pinner's-brow on the 6 h June. P.O Beach-on proved the case, and tho defendant was fined 53. and costs.—Peer Cotton, Winwick-st was charged with having been drunk and oisorderly on the 6th June, Fined half-a c-own, including coats. COMMITTING A BREACH OF IRE PRACE.—John Cunning- ham an-1 Thomas Moran, Barrack-st., were charged with committing a breach of the peace on the 6th inst.—P.C. Beacham proveel th case, and said he saw the d-fendants fighting in Crown-st. —They were fined 33. 63. each. _ . . _</p>
        <p>c'FURIOUS DRIVING.—AIfred Lowe, Acton Bridge, was charged with !uriously 'driving a horse and cart in Bridge-st. on the Bth inst.—P.C. Cooper proved the charge, and said he saw the defendant in chare of a horse and cart going at about twelve miles an hour down Bri ige-st. towards Warring- ton Bridge—P.C. Whitaker corroborated.—Defendant pleaded that he tried his best to stop the horse, and had ,the reins tight in his hands.—A witness named Moore said he saw the d,fendant's horse going at a quick p,ce down Bridge-st., but to his mind the horse started on the gallop when a 'bus was passing.—Defendant was tined five shillings and costs. CASE DISMISSED.—John Appleton was charged with furi- ously riding all rse in Winwick-st • on the Bth June.—P.C. Clark stated the facts, and the magistrates dismissed the case oa acco,krir of the unmanage ableness of the horse. A MORALIZING FEMALE —Kate Ann Jones. a factory hand, was charged with having beer drunk and disorderly on Town-lull, on the 6th June.—P.C. Cooper proved the charge. —Prisoner said she did not kniis what she said when she was in dunk—Alderman Sutton Why do you drink ? Prisoner : I get into compafiy. I hive very seldom any money to get drink. If you always get me up for being drunk you will have one of the Fyist in Warrington.—The Chief Constable: When Kate Ann Jones uses bad language it is had language.—Prisoner: L shall go away.—The Chief Constable: And coate baek to-morrow.—She was fined ss. and costs,</p>
        <p>TUF-SDA.Y.—Before Mr. Robert Garnett (chairman), and Mr. R•</p>
        <p>J. Guest. Two TERRYS IN THE COURT. —ll man named Samna Terry, 32, Watburton-st., was charged with assaulting his wife, Ellen Terry on the 13th June.—Comolainant who bade b'ack eye, saiclbn th 3 night of the day in question defendant came home about 5 minutes to 12, and after .ome words struck her in the, mouth. He got her n tho corner and jumped on her.—Mary Jane Green said that on the same night she he trd a scream of "murder." and when she got outside complainant's house she saw defendant drag her out of bed. He struA her and then jumped on her chest. Wit- ness saw this through the windew o the ground floor.—De- fendant denied the assault, and alleged violence on the tart of his wife.—He was committed for o e month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>DRINK WAS VTR DA.UBK OF IT ALL.—Thornis Oxley, 22. Mason-st., wa chargel with assaulting and 14ea.ting his wif,, Ann Oxley, on the 13th June.—Defendant pleaded guilty.— Mrs. Oxley said defend .nt when he was drunk had a very bad temp.r. On the day in question he was drunk. When he opened the door she 'threw a book towards him. She had a nasty temper herself, stie• admitted. Defendant came in and met her with a jog which he struck her on the head with. She had to have her head dressed by a Doctor. She would</p>
        <p>like a separation owing to defendant's viol sage —The Chief Constable said the man was in a fearful state through drink, he could neither keep an eye or limb still.—Complain. an' in an,,wer to the chairman. said it would never have hap- pened but for drink. —Defendant w.is committed for smooth. —Complainant then relented and t aid she did nct desire the separation. A VIOLENT PBTSONER.—Wi ham Dociwell, 33. Winwick-st., was charged with being drunk aid disorderly in Dial-erect, on Monday night. —P.C. Deau proved the case.—lt was further stated Cara defendant had been behaving like a mad- man in the cells.—P.C. Marsh said he had had to go to defett ant 12 times. lie Lad also kicked the prisoner Oxley. —Defendant was sent to gaol fir one month's ha-4 labour. TROUBLESOME LABoURERS A RalcUE.—Larrrence Man- nion, Howard-street, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Wilders,col-road at 430 on MotVay afternoon. William Kelly, 9. Bennett-street, was charged with rescuing Mannion from the custody of the police. P.C. Berry said that on the afternoon of ildonday he was on duty at Bridge Foot and slw Mannion there. Re was working with a gang of PavlourS on the road and WM drunk and very disorderly. Witness went to him and prisoner challeqqed him to fight Witness put him on the ground and Kelly came up and pulled him away, with the result that Mannion escaped Witness tiot assistance and wrested both men.—P.C. Edwards corroborated as to Mannion's drinakenness.—James He 'r. Broadbeot. manager for the Bridge-foot Ito • es. corrobor- ated Berry's statement.—Both prisoners were fined 13s. and costs.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL CPWR.—The following persons were fined for not senillinq their children regolarly to school : John Barnes, Ireland's-yard. Cros.ley_str,.et, wAs fined 2s. 6d ; Charles Campbell. 3. Bake-house-yard. 23 ; Michael Dempsey, 18. Dutton-street, 23. 61. ; James Slolly, 2, Back Crossley street. 2s. 61.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDLY.:-Bofnr. Mr. John Pgirolmvzh (chairman). Mr.</p>
        <p>j Geddes and 16. J. IT. Fl;minw. TRE RESCVF. FRoM THRme-E.—John Daley was charged on a warrant with nnlaw fully striking P.O. John Berry whilst in the execution of his dutv.—Tho Chief Constable said that defendant was the man who assisted in the rescue of Mannion from VII constable. in a case which was dealt with the provions day.—P.C. Berry proved the ca e and said that prisoner pulled him away from Mannion and scratched his neck while so-doing.—Pined 110, and costs or 11 days. "A H,111131? or N tiCED SAVAORR " IN Trig TOWN.—Ttlins Htir.t. a youth, was charged with eTnosing his person in Winwick-road on Tuesday morning —P.O. Taylor stated that at 11 o'plock on Tuesday morning; he was on duty in Winwirtic-road when he received several complaints from persons In the neighbourhood, ef men and youths hathinq in a pond close by. He proc.eded there and saw de- fendsnt and a number of other youths hathinz. Witness caught defendrnt and questioned him. He said he had no home. The nond w,a about 20 yards from the road —The</p>
        <p>Chief Constable said he had hi ft number a complaints dur-</p>
        <p>ing the hot weather of people bathing in the ponds. Between the Cheshire Lines and Bewsey it was frightful, the bathers were "a horde of • eked savagea."—Deferviant pro- mised not to b,the in the nnnds again and was ordered to be discharged on the rising of the Court. DRITIVIC AND DIAMMF.RLY.—nertAiti DelftriPy was charged with helot,: cir,lnk ani di.nrderly in Flutterrnarket-et., on Tuesday evening.—He we fined ss. including costs.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNUCWAY.fere vlr Jr,fin White (chairman). Mr. Z. Arreltsce, Mr. W. Roue titan, and Mr. J. Tinsley</p>
        <p>DRUNKFIfWV.SR.—For beim/ drunk awl disorderly on the 7th June. Jnhn Ntigont. Peulton. was finf</p>
        <p>CYCLING ON TIM. FnOTPAYIL—Arthur Whiftle, 'Tarring- ton, was tined is. and new, for riciinz hieyele on the foot- path in iroarnheaci-road. Poelton, on the 9th Jnne.—On Liverpool-road. San'ey. the same rft..ne, was comm;ll,4 on the 12th Jone by Pleh rd Wright and James Wrw.—Defend- ants were fined 4s. and costs.-I,nr the same off.nee on the same road on the 12th, Freder-ek Appleton was fined ss. and swots. •</p>
        <p>Orystafen AukiNST THE CANAL BOATS AM—Willi/Mr- Burtpn. canal boat own, r, was eha-ged, belie th,s owner of the renal boat "Florence." with haying allowed it to be used as a dwelling house without its being regi-tered ; John Costello, to stet of the boat WAS charged with using it as a dwelling. Mr Burton pleaded guilty, but Costello did not appear.—Mr. Kerfoot (inspector) said that, he inspected the boat in the Sankey Canal. It was being used as a dwelling- honse. He made enquiries and found that It was not regis- tered —Defendant Burton said the boat was only used tem- porarily as one had been stink.—A. fine of 10s, including costs was imposed on each of the defendants. Wiyv MAINTENANCE. —Arthur Honvhton. Newton, was summoned for the non-maintenance of his wife.—Mr. Shearer, enquiry officer. Warrington. said the parties had been married five years and had separased three times. On the occasion complainent applied to the Guardians and def--nriant last promised them that he would give her 4s.a week He had not done so. and the Guardians supp,srted her.—Mrs. Hough- ton corroborated and said defendant had c,ontrihnted nothing towards her support since the 7th March —Defendant was ordered to pay 4s. a week towards his wife's maintenance</p>
