      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Berore Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman) awl</p>
        <p>Colonel Wood</p>
        <p>A. BATCH OF GAMVSTER4.—Arthtir Lee, Charles Lee.. Tames Lee. .Tosenh Mel-oven. James Vernon, Nathaniel Taylor. William Blower. :Tames Aldrid ,e. William Wilson, and Frank Hall all of Earlestown were charged with gaming on the 7th June.—Defendants pleaded g iltv with the exception of Wilson who did not appear.—P CI Schofield stated that at three o'clock, on Sunday. the 7th inst., he saw the defend- ants playing " banker" behind Junction-lane. Rarlestown. The po ice h•d ree,sivecl several complaints. He watched the defendants for half-an-hour. when on seeing him they de- camped. H. picked up the cants (produced) and sixpence In eopper.—P.C. Borne gave carroborative evidence.—Wilson was fined Is. and costs, and the other defendants the costs, 6s 64.</p>
        <p>A Vir()A(E!ei QUARREL.—Eliz‘beth H. Wilson D-rhy.st.. Earlestown, W 45 charged with haying on the 3tsth May et Newton committed wilful damage at, the house of Ann Battersby. Derby-street. by breaking a quantity of crockery - ware. v hie 12s - Ann Battersby deposed that on the date named the defendant entered her house anti threw a quantity oc glasses and ornaments at her and broke them. The articles were on a table in wit- ness's house and she estimated the damage at 12s. —J. Baldwin. Hotel-street. spoke to seeing the defendant come from the complainant's house He .aw the broken things on the floor.—Rilzabeth McMahon and amen named Sndlow also give evidence —The defendant denied that, she threw any of the articles at the complainant. She stated that the complainant assaulted her. and that sh a (witness) lost h-r</p>
        <p>temper. The complainant knocked the things off the table herself. The magtstrAtel cm,idereel that it was a case of six ot one and half-a-dozen ot another and dismissed it.</p>
        <p>A DRUNKEN' S kILOR AT EAR LEF,ToWN.—Christopher Peterson, a sailor of Russian Finland, was charged with be- ing drunk and disorderly the previous night at Earlestown, and also with committing wilful damage to the extent of 16s. V.,(1., by breaking the windows of the house of Eli Bunting, Prisoner, who had a fairly good knowledge of English said he knew nothing about it. He admitted that, he was drunk, PC. Schofield stated that at a quarter past eight the previous night, the prisoner was drunk in Railway-st., Earlestown, and was without trousers, coat, and hat, lie broke the windows at the back of Mr Bunting's house. Be had picked up the tiles in the yard and thrown them through the window. He was taken to the police station, and a ticket was found in the prisoner's possession which had bean issued at Liverpool that day for South Shields. Thu prissmer appeared to have got off the train at Earlestown —Prisoner stated that he had •-.o money in his possession, but twelve months wages were due to him at North Shields. H landed in Liverpool the previous day, and the Board of Trade a ivancel him Lila order to get to Shields. He got into the wrong train and got out at Earlestown.—ln answer to the Clerk, he said he would be willing that the police should receive the money for the damage he had done, out of his w ,ges.—Tne magistrat s thereupoo dis •barged the prisoner on condition that the damages should be paid, .</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DniionnEßLY.—Thomas Plinston, Spibey, and William Hayes, were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk and disorderly at Golborne on the sth June. Patrtck Carney wis fined ss. and costs and Anthony Carney 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Turton: stre,t. Go'borne, c the 6:h inst.,</p>
        <p>_ BRF.I.CH OF TH9' PEAC —Richard Jackson, Henry Jackson. and Frederick Gaiter of Hayelock, were all charged with corn - mitring a breach of the peace at Haydock on the 6th lot. and were bound over in their own recognizances of £5 to keep the peace for six months._</p>
        <p>BRUTAL ASSAULT ON A. GOLBoRNE FARMER. -Joseph Woods, employed at Bold Colliery and residing at Golborne. was charged with having on the sth June assaulted and beaten Edwatsf Last. There was also a cross-summons issued by Woods, charging Edward Last with assault—Mr. Stuart. solicitor, stated that he appe,recl on behalf of Mr. Last, who was a farmer, residing at Golborne. In order to save a longer round to his work, Woods had been in the habit of trespass- ing on Mr. Last's farm. Mr. Last had warned Woods on previous occasions but that h.d had no effect upon him. About 7.30 on the stls inst., Woods repeated the trespass and Mr. Last proceeded to go after him in order to ascertain his name and addrees for the purpose of taking proceedings against him. When he overtook Woo is he put his hand on his shoulder and asked him his name. Woods without any further communication hit Mr. Last on the s"de of the head and knocked him down. Mr. Last was somewhat stunned but managed to follow after Woods and get up to him just as he got through the fence in Barn-lane. He endeavoured to get hold of Woods. teat Woods being in an advai tagsous posi- tion struck out over the fence and hitihis (Mr. Stuart's) client over the eye, cutting it badly and causing blood to flow Woods had no authority to walk across the field and he, Mr. Stuart, urged for a severe penalty to be inflicts-ad.—Edward Last farmer, Golborne, corrohorated his soli itor's statement. He denied th it he struck Woods or that he set the do at him. He was not drunk.--Buth Bullock, residing with Mr Last, stated that she saw Woods commi.. the assault. She WAS about 60 Tar Is away. Mr. Last did not strike Woods, but got hold of him by the co,lar.—William Mitchell also gave evi- dence for the prosecution and characterised the assets' t as a most cowardly one. —For the defence Woo +is denied that he ever struck the comp'ainant. The injuries were caused by his falling against the fence. He admi ted that he WAS *rea- m. sing.—Themagistrates dismissed the Case against Edward Last, and fined Woods 20s. and costs. ASSAULT ON 1 GREENGR,CER —Alfrad Twist was summoned on a charge f having at Golberne assault-d Mary Jane Tay- lor.—M..Willet, solieit Warrinston.prosecuted. He stated that the compiainant was a greengrocer, and she was going down Lowton-road the previous S durday morning with a cartioad of vegetables,when the defendant came up to her and used most filthy language. She told him she would make him pay for it. and he told her that if there had been no one about, he would have muriered her. On going back that way later in the day, he struck her on the face several times. The complainant corroborated Mr. Willet's statement, and stated that the defendant climbed on the wheels of the cart In order to strike her.—Richard Jones, James Henri Hind- les% and James Eckersley also gave evidence. The defendant contended that, be was asmulted.-1 he magistrates said they were convinced that an assault was committed, and would fine the elelendant les. and costs. THEFT Er 1 LAD.—Walter Westhesd, a lad. 10 years of age, of Havdock, was charged with stealing the sum of Es. 6,1. In money belonging to James Norcross —The complainant stated that lie was an engine winder and lived with his mother at 9, Chiptiley-lane, Havdosk. On the 3rd June the defendant came to their house and said he had no home. The defendant was allowed to sleen in witness's room Witness had a money-box in his bedroom, and it contained Bs. 6d. The defsndant saw wit less put some money in the b The following day he found the money had been taken from his box, and the defendant was missing. He gave information to the police.—John Martin, living at Mrs Cassidy's lo tging house. Sr. Helens, spoke to the defendant coming to him on St. Helens station. He asked him if he could lodge with him. They spent some money together. -P C. Davies, of Haydock, spoke to apprehending the defendant at St. Helens. He was selling evening newspapers, arid had Is. 11d. In his possession. On being charged he admitted stealing the money at Haydock. He was ordered to receive six strokes of the birch rod.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MoNnaY.—Eefore Messrs. Brundrit and H. Reynolds.</p>
        <p>LIcF.YstNo BUSINESS.—AIbert Griffiths applied for tempor- ary authority to sell in the °eh:Fellows Arms, Wellington-at., lately occupied by P. Hurst. Henry Moreton made a similar application to sell at the Millstone Inn. Mill-street, lately occupied by J. Woodrow —The applications were granted A SLEEPY DWYER. —William Bebbington was summoned for teing in charge ea horse and cart at Aston on June 10th without having proper control over the horse. The evidence given was that defendant wee fast asleep in the cart. Deb- b'ngton pleaded guilty and was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—SatnueI Anderson was fined ss. for being drunk in Mersey-st., on June 18th ; Samuel Higgins, was fined 5s for being drunk in the same street on the same date; Philip Samson was fined ss. for being drunk in High- it., on June 14th. A VIOLENT MENDICINT.—WiIIiam Daweey, of no address. was c'orged with begging in A,hr,dge-st on Saturday night. and also at the same time and Wane, wilfully commit- ting damage to a plate glass window the property of Messrs. Greenail Whitley A'Co. to the extent of £s.—The landlorst of the Bridge Inn.Mr.Riley said that pi-ismer called in his house on Saturslay night and he noticed him soliciting alms. He did not say anything to the prisoner until he be-ame offen- sive, when he had t 3 tell him to desist, end as prisoner te- came abusive, he was turned out. Outside. the prisoner seemed to have had a scuffle with someone.and shortly after- wards he threw a bottle through a plate glass lettered win- dow.—P.C. Gregory said that when he visited the house, there was a crowd of about 200 people round defendant, who was using very bad language.—Defendant was sent to gaol for begging for 14 days. and for'committing damage to the window was ordered a month's imprisonment. ASSAULT ON CYCLISTS BY IRISPIMEN.-- JAMES Frain and Michael Sherlock, two Irish farm labourers, were charged with being drunk and disorderly at. Aston on Saturday night. Frain was also charged with assaulring John Craw( rd. and Sherlock with assaulting John Davies; both the complainants being Runcorn cyclists.—Thornas Crawford said that he went for a short ride with several friends on Sunday evening. On returning after ten in the evening they came op with the defend into on the highway between Run- corn and Aston. They rang their bells but the men in the road refused to move and one of them struck Davies He (witness) to avoid running into the man, dismounted, and on getting on the bicycle again was ' flocked down by Frain into the dite.h at the roadside. He was of opinion Unit the men were drunk —John Davies gave evidence to the same effect and stated that he was struck by the prisoner, Sherlock. when they got up with the Irishmen. He also saw Crawford assaulted.—PC 's Benfield and Ridgway gave evidence on the charge of drunkenness.—Mr. Brundrit in giving the magistrates' decision, said it was a serious matter for the prisoners, to b ock up the highway as they did. The matter might have ended in serious injury to the cyclists. They would each have to en to gaol for 14 days on tbe charge of being drunk and for one month on the charge of assault</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WONDERFUL FOOD BEVERAGE.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL FOOD BEVERAGE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Tit:Wes' Vi•Cnena as a fond Beveraee possesses Nutrient. Restorative, and Vitalising Properties, which have hitherto been nnn-exieent. MEDICAL EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>The" British Medical Journal " save :—" Vi-Cocoa is a very palatable beverage of great stimulating and sus- taininz properties."</p>
        <p>The" Lancet" says :—" Vi-Cocol. must be assigned a place in the front rank of really valuable foods, since it is the embodiment of the numerous principles contained in Malt, flops, Kila.and Cocoa. . . . . of distinct value as a restorative and stimulative food." From G H. Haslam, Eq., M R C.S.. L.S.A. "It gives me great pleasure in bearing tegtimonv to the value of Vi-Cocna, a mixture of Malt, Hops, Kola, and Caracas Cocoa Extract. I consider it the very b.t pre- paration of the kind in the market and, as a nourishing drink for children and adults, the finest that has ever been brought before the public. As a general beverage it excels all previous preparations. No house should be without it."</p>
        <p>It aids the dizestive nnwers, and is invaluable e peci- allr at this season to tired men and delicate women and children.</p>
        <p>It has the refreshinz praperties of hne tea, the nour- ishment of the hnst cocoas, and a tonic and recuperative force possessed by neither, and can be used in all cases where tea and coffAe arm prohibited.</p>
        <p>- It is not a medicine, but a unique and wonderful Summer Food Beverage. The wonderful African Kola nut which it contains has concentrated powers of nutriment, and imparts stamina and physical powers, adds to power of endurance, and enables ttio.n who use it to undergo greater physical exertion and fatigue.</p>
        <p>Dr. Tritf•leg' Vi-Coca is made up in 6. packsts, and 91. and is. 6d. tins. It can he obtained front all chemists, grocers, and storm or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk House, Cannon-street, London, E C.</p>
        <p>With the main object of still further making known to the nublio the manifold advantages of so palatable and refreshine• a beverage, we are prepared to send to any reader who names the Warrington Examiner a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa tree and post paid.</p>
        <p>The file cutters of Manchester and district, owing to the briskness of trade, are pressing their employers to return to the " full price" which was departed from over 20 rears ago.</p>
        <p>THE DUET OF ITA.Nrrr;rox writes :—" I have been and am quite satisfied with ICops Ale, and think it an exce lent beverage."—January 17th, H 393. THE IiIImHOF OF W %Kr:FIELD sags Kops Ale is an excellent beverage." Fifteen Gold Medals Awarded. Two million bottles said in one week. Bittered from choicest Hops only. Agent :—W. FuIINESS, Rep's Stores, Bewsey-.it., Warrington. (7)</p>
      </div>
