        <p>TAal BOWLING.</p>
        <p>HATDOCK V. PILKINGTON'S PLATE GLASS WORKS.— Played at Klaydock on Saturday afternoon, Haydock winning easily by 97 pOints• Scores : Halrnocs. PLATE: WORKS. Inn Jackson l5 W R Thomson J Williams l5 J Hurst It Whittle l5 T Price ll A Shaw T Borry l5 P Heaton lO W Pedley l4 Jag Ashall inn Baines J Haselden D Livesley H Pilling W Marsh W Smith Yas Baines l5 B Siddeley J Mar.h T Davies Jno Millington R Hazlehurst W Beattie l5 It Hirch 8 Plant G Pimblett J Bibby l4 W Ash t11. J Tither . 15 C Lawrensen B Twist 5 W johnsin .-</p>
        <p>J Berry 4 l5 W Fletcher lO Jas Pilling l5 Jos Swann 7 R Fairhurst Jos Davies l5 P Neville 6</p>
        <p>283 97 majority for Haydoek.</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>A. CONSTABLE AND CO. —Nella Parker,hasi made a good bid for popularity by her authorship of " Dramas of to Day," a book of short stories. There are many " books of short stories " and the description does not convey much but in this case the stories are of exceptional merit. Each is brief and possesses one or more incidents of dramatic force. Though short each littie tale abounds in pathos and feeling and all are truly artistic. The inci- dents are' dram is" of every day description and their at- tractiveness lies in the vivid presentation of them by the writer. Different to the remainder is " Four Points of View," the first of the items in the book. The governess of a family gets married and discusses her own position, with herself. This chapter is entitled "In the School- room." She and her admirer are also" taken stock of" "In the nursery,"" In the kitchen," and "In the Drawing Room." The four little sketches are cleverly drawn and rather amusing at times. The reader of " Dramas of to day" will be impelled to favour Nella Parker with the comment that her production gives promise of greater things. Messrs. Constable and Co., the publishers, have issued the book in a cover of tasteful design enclosing reading matter printed in large type on good paper.—Another of the " Acme Library " series by these publishers is " A Feminine Conviction," by George St. George. Practically there are only two characters in the story, but both are excellent. One, an American girl, com- mits a crime, in her eyes justifiable, but English law sends her to prison. While here, her courageous bear- ing and self-absolution win the love of the chaplain,who on her releave persuades her to marry him. A short spell of happiness is granted them and then the thunder- bolt falls, with unlooked for and terrible result. The d.-mouement is entirely in keeping wi.h English police law as at present enforced, but the reader is to ced to look upon it from a much different stand- point by George St. George's book. " A Femi- nine Conviction " is simple but honestly clever.— Cricket is now in full swing and therefore a book of cricket songs, by Norman Gale, should be all the more acceptable. The songs are in a lively vain and embody the ideals and ideas of all cricketers. Some verses give advice, some praise players, and some aro parodies, but all will prove attractive to the devotees of stumps, bat, and ball.</p>
        <p>SAXON AND CO.—These publishers are well known as the producers of countless little reference books on many topics, and ior many uses. From their offices we have received a "Bible Dictionary" which, we should think. will be useful almost everywhere, but more particularly in the Sunday school, to clergymen of all denominations, and to all kinds of theological students. The dictionary contains over 400 pages and explains fully the meanings of things, the names of places and pers3ns, &amp;c., peculiar to Biblical lore. Readers of the Bible, by the help of this dictionary, shculd be able to explain away many of the problems they have hitherto puzzled over. Bound in two styles and of handy form, this new dictionary should command an extensive sale. It is guaranteed by the publishers to be accurate and carefully compiled. MACMILLAN AND CO.—III the Tennyson People's Edition the final two volumes of Idylls of the King have been issued this month.—Philip Augustus forms the current issue or Biographies of Foreign Statesmen. Mr. W. H. Hutton, 8.D., is the biographer,and his narrative of the reign of this French sovereign is as complete as that of Richelieu which preceded it. Mr. Hutton has also related his facts in an interesting and graphic manner. In the concluding chapter of his book he says of Philip, " Great as a conqueror,he was even greater in constructive and unifying power. What he found he consolidated and what he founded he laid firm. . • . In his work and in himself he is worthy to take his Diace among the great statesmen who have made the Europe of to-day."—Jane Austen's "Sense and Sensi- bility "is the latest out of the Illustrated Standard Novels. Mr. Austin Dobson supplies the introduction and compares " Sense and Sensibility " to o ber works of the same author and those of ether writers published about the same time. It is clear trial Mr. Dobson has made himself thoroughly conversant with the merits and demerits of the works Of Jane Austen and her contem- poraries. Excellent and natural illustrations from the pencil of Hugh Thomson considerably enhance the book.</p>
        <p>CASSELL AND Co.—This firm is always favour- ing the public with cheaper editions of some of their high class works. Of late, there has been a number of these, and two more are to hand. One is Cassell's Natural History which has started in sixpenny weekly parts, to he complete in 26. Altogether there will be about 2.000 illustrations, and a series of new full-page plates is being expressly prepared for the edition.—Cas- sel' Book of Sports and Pastime., is being issued in penny parts, and should at once attain popularity with the laver of British games.—With this month the fifth and last number of Royal Academy Pictures comes be- fore the pu bile. As previously announced,with this part is included a special plate of Sir John Millais' picture " A Foro-runner." It is a beautifully toned reproduction, and well-worthy of forming the frontpiece ;to the bound volume of Royal Academy Pictures. The usual intro- ductory review of the pictures in the five parts is in- cluded in tke last number.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Westerby, of Manchester, presided at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. He de- livered a brief address in which he urged his hearers to put their best in their work. Mr. James Evans, of Warrington, was the speaker for the afternoon. The subject of hi 3 address was" Manhood and Character." He spoke of character being the most priceless of posses- sions, and stated that a man's influence did net cease with his death. The address was listened to with close attention, and the speaker was able to add point to his remarks by relating some of his own experiences. Mrs. R. H. Pearson was the soloist, and she gave an exquisite rendering of " The Worker." Mr. Thomas Wallington, also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>The Baroness de Valley, a lady of eighty-two, who lived alone at 26, Rue de Penthievre, Paris, was found dead on Tuesday afternoon in her room. She had been gagged and throttled. Robbery seems to have been the motive of the murder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Beard was held in Bewsey Cham- bers, Warrington, on Wednesday afternoon. Captain Withington occupied the chair, and there were present Messrs. R. Garnett, J.P., William Houghton, J.P., James Houcyhton, R. B. Matthews.H. Thornton, W. Fairclough, H. Bate, PA. Bate, J. Clare, Joshua Ball and E. A. White (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SURVEYOR.</head>
        <p>THE SURVEYOR.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor (Mr. Thompson) wrote to the Chairman saying he would not be able to attend the meeting owing to illneAs.—The CHAIRMAN said they all regretted that Mr. Thompson was unable to be present.</p>
        <p>THE AUDIT.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that the annual audit of the accounts of the Board had been made, and they were found to be correct.</p>
        <p>BARNES' BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The SonvEyoa, in his report concerning this bridge at Great Sankey, ,stated that the widening of it was in hand. HA was waiting for steel girders and these were expected to arrive in a few days when the completion of the work would be proceeded with.--Mr. Bate (Great Sankey) said the work seemed to be proceeding very satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TENDERS FOR MATERIAL.</head>
        <p>TENDERS FOR MATERIAL.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the Surveyor the following tenders were accepted :—Macadam delivered at Collins Green, Warrington Central, Padgate, Glazebrook, Cut- cheth, Lowton.St. Mary's. Messrs. Darbis.,ire's,Lintited ; delivered at Lowton Junction, Kenyon. Glazebury.Fid- dler's Ferry, Bank Quay, Sankey, Messrs. Brundrit aid Company ; setts and kerbs, Messrs. Walton Bros. ; socket pipes, Messrs. Doulton and Company ; tiles (deli- vered at the Eastern side of the district), Mr. Lewis Bur- chall ; delivered at the western s de,the executors of the late Mr. James Warburton; cast iron gullies, Mr. James Smethurst ; spades, Mr.H.N. Houghton ; shovels,Messrs. Caleiwells, Limited ; engine oil, Messrs. Spurrier, Glaze- brook and Company.—lt was pointed out by the Chair- man that there was only one tender for socket pipes. The Warrington Slate Company had been asked to submit a tender, but it had not been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>REPAIR OF COUNTY COUNCIL ROADS.</head>
        <p>THE REPAIR OF COUNTY COUNCIL ROADS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK asked for authority to attach the seal of the Board to an agreement with the County Council to repair main roads for that body for the sum of £2,425, including £l2O for establishment charges, and a special grant of £737 for improvement in Winwick•road at a cost of £305 ; Leigh road £330, and Lowton road £lO2. It was stated that the County Council grant was the same as last, year.—On the motion of Mr. James Hoagh- bin, seconded by Mr. R. Garnett, the seal of tha Board was attached to the agreement.</p>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC reported that the balance in hand amounted to £823 14s. 4d. Burtonwood and Kenyon had already paid their June calls.--The Chairman said the balance last year was £722 is. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEW ROAD AT ARPLET.</head>
        <p>THE NEW ROAD AT ARPLET.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATTHEWS said he went over the road at Arpley about the repair of which they had had a dispute with the Manchester Ship Canal Coinpany,and he was glad to say that the Warrington Corporation were fulfilling their duties with regard to it, and were spending the £250 received from the Manchester Ship Canal Company. They had put one end of the road in repair, and he thought in about six weeks' time it would be thoroughly repaired.—The Chairman said it was a very wise policy on the p of the Corporation because they would be taking the road over when the Borough Extension Bill was passed.—Mr. Matthews said the Board would have no fuither difficulties in the matter.</p>
        <p>The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>RAND REEFS.</p>
        <p>There has been great activity in the shares of this company during the last few days, and we are not sur- prised at this, as the possibilities of this concern shculd bring about a very considerable rise in the shares. Last year the price stood very much higher than their present quotation of about 12s. The company owns 115 valu- able claims about four miles south,of Johannesburg,and is surrounded by some of Henderson's properties, which have been most favourably reported on. The Manager, in the latest reports, states he has struck a new reef on the property, measuring Bft. wide, which give 14dwt. to the ton. In addition to this experts have given their opinion that the deep level of the main reef runs through the entire property. As a speculative investment we think thee shares can be bought with advantage and should be one of the first to advance in the African market.—" BULLIONIST," June 13th.</p>
        <p>From the observations taken at Wood Abraham's es- tablishment,Lord-st.,Liverpool, it appears that between one and two o'clock en Tuesday afternoon the thermome- ter registered 87 degrees in the shade, the tempera- ture in the sun being 106 degrees. This is the highest yet recorded this season. On Sunday the temperature was 84, and cn Monday 83, in the shade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
