      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>A RECORD MONTH'S TRAFFIC. The official return of the total weight of merchao.,dil passing through the Manchester Ship Canal during 18 establishes a new record, and eh e fact that the hieb total attained last year (viz.. 133.665 tons in Octri has been thrice surpassed during the last five m00th4.4 distinctly encouraging. A circumstance which read% last month's record particularly satisfactory is that bg May the cotton traffic, which swelled the returns for to winter months, was comparatively trifling. owing season being practically over, whint the timber tr° which forms a very large proportion of the canal's bo ness during the summer, was only beginning, and 'f undoubtedly assume much greater dimensions dur,l°a; the next two or three months. These consideratiajo seem to point to a steady increase in the general trl4 which is unaffected by seasonal influences, and rendll it practically certain that when the timber trade 04f full swing. even last month's figures will be greiboif exceeded. The appended table illustrates the groWtb the traffic during the current year : Seagoing. Total. Tons. Tons. January February 103.273 March April 113.993 May llO 241 It is noteworthy that the March traffic, the smallest file recorded this year, was nearly 53 per cent, more than</p>
        <p>_ traffic for the corres:ionding month of last year. LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC. During the week ended Juno 12 the number ef going cargo trissels arriving at and sailing from the po of Manches t, ' sr was 193.reoresentin an aggregate heV.,ll; gistered tonnage nf 47.884 tons In the preceding wna the figures wee 154 ships, 40.235 tons. The coaFtvf,:; traffic was accountable for 159 ships of 27,376 t o cvsitos compared with 124 ships of 2L986 tons in the:din the week,whilst 34 ships of 20.488 tons were eng4g,rhe foreign trade.against 30 ships of 18,249 NAIL ritee ~, ports included cltton and grain from Galveston, Pyfroto from Spain,cotton and onions fr)ro Euivot,logw°°a Laguna, and fustic from San Juan del Sur, timber so/ wood pulp from Norway. Sweden and Russia. flints ft?' France, new potatoes from Jersey, and general.Jargc the regular weekly steamers from various Contirit4 ports. The outward foreign traffic,in addition to ti gular weekly services, included sailings toAugt.o Alexandria, Amsterdans,Norwegian and Baltic port's Petersburg, and Cette. ,</p>
        <p>THE JERSEY POTATO TRAFFIC. „mtild The efforts now being made to Permanently m—sto ips the new potato traffic from the Channel Islands for Ship Canal appear to be meeting with an encourago, degree of success, notwithstanding the fact that a Ceel% peting service has been started :unning twice a 'tool between Jersey and Liverpool. The returns for WOO four weeks of the present season, which began on, oil 11, afford striking evidence uf the preponderance o'lo shipments to Manchester as compared with those aro, ports. During the neriod named 21 steamere o'ol Manchester,Bomhay,and General Navigation CoMPB,7BO line landed 66,323 packages from St. Helier ; steamers brought 26,293 packages to Fleetwood, /f'cri the shipments to Liverpool by eight steamers *wools to only 20,308 packages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A. FORGE LLBOURB/ of On Saturday, Will'am laolden, aged 55 yeoll Brookside Villa, Station-road, Penketh, expir! de the Warrington Infirmary. It appears that rop ceased worked as a forge labourer in the sett, forge,Bewsey, and when in the pay office ab°ubi receive his wages on Saturday, he was notice 0.0,, James Holt, foreman fitter, residing at Priestlef,co to stagger, as if in a fit. Dr. Adams, medical 01' to of the works, was sent for, and ordered Holdell '0 the Infirmary, where he died. Whil,t in the its:lo, tution he was attended by the senicr house sarg7,lo Dr. Langdale, *IQ certified death to be due t'io, cerebral hemorrhage and kidney disease. 5°</p>
        <p>quest will be held: WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB. p 004 Through the courtesy of Mr. John White, J.. the energy of Mr. E. E. Lowe (leader),the metti-ef 140 of the Field Club had an opportunity on Sate „0 of visiting a portion of Woolston Moss er the adjacent plantations. There was a good il'ot dance and some valuable captures were madeotl,, few of which will in due time go to enrlePoo Natural History collections of our Museum. hitherto unrecorded species of plants were, 0.1 botanical contribution to the day's work,whilsvetif entomologists were profitably busy with their 0.180 and two spiders new to the district were 010' taken (Tetragnatha extensa andTrochosa terse bell The members returned by 'bus tired with t exertions, but well pleased with the result. A MAD DOG AT LARGS. Some little commotion was caused in 3-1 street on Sunday morning, by the fact that 3 oft! dog was running about the street. It appeersanie a gentleman, unknown, was walking accoraP.7, by a gentleman friend with two fox terriers osoo vicinity of Factory-yard. There the gentlew dog suddehly sprang at his master's clothes- r, or animal was shaken off and gripped by the collaroot was led into Milner-street,where,an immense et vsit: congregated. The dog appeared to be in greL'al3i and foamed at the mouth. A revolver was on the scene, but the man who attempted too sit, the dog, fired the contents of the pistol in td and the dog still howled. Someone suggeete care," it should be drowned. A tub was then Pr°ooll7 the animal immersed—still howling, and drowned. E EVERY CYCLIST should use JOHN S NEW ROAD MAP,6O miles about Warring, at 34, Bridge-st and 23,Butt-rrnarket-st.—Advc,',.q. 07' A WARRINGTONCLERGYMAN PUBLISHES BANNS. An d, An unusual incident occurrel in St. Annr6 Winwick-road, Warrington, on SundaY 11,317j30,13; It appears that the curate ,the Rev. p.."..„ Jou; intends to marry a Manches 4 lady earlY 111`1,, 00'5 and the duty devolved uponhim to publish ono. banns of marriage. This', he heroicall9 Uto thse another interesting feature of the event 17„ the future bride was in the church at the time. irotr IRON and BRASS BEDSTEADS.—GARLANV' • monger, Warrington. —Advt. it.ceerlie Mrs PONSONBY'S HAIR REsTonart, a. great Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innaaerooe Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In gre3t.,t, by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow. Prev%i 00.01, nese, Invigorating, restores hair to its uatur-astorljoi, gives healthy and youthful appearince 14°5'5g-to. ing results ; 2s 6d Bottle, post tree.—Address sr‘ SONDY. 25, Lime-st.. Liverpool—Advt. L A.WN MOWERS. —G xnLAND, Bridge-street, rington,—Advt.</p>
