      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY &amp;T EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASION AL CORRESPONDE NT.</p>
        <p>The gamblers who have for some time past desecrated the Sabbath by playing cards for money, in what they supposed were quiet, retired out-ot- the-way places have been visited by the far reach- ing strong arm of the law. They have pursued their avocation with immtmity from disturbance, but at last the police swooped down, and in fisher phrase, made a tine haul, and the county is a trifle richer thereby. I hope the police will follow up their successes on this occasion, and come down heavy on the betting fraternity who assemble day after day in the vicinity of Mort's farm, raking in from foolish women the hard earned money of their husbands.</p>
        <p>I regret to learn that the committee which was formed to carry out a Re Queen Festival at Ilay• dock, have found themselves compelled to abandon the idea of holding a festival this year. The chief stumbling block I believe is the field, which is in the occupation of the llaydock Football Club, and about whic3 the two committees cannot come ito terms. It is a pity some arrangement cannot bear- rived at, as the festival is eagerly looked forward to by the children.</p>
        <p>The bathing season is now in full swing, and I am afraid many anxious moments will be spent by the parents, for the salety of their children. The principal bathing places about here are the Canal and the Common Pit, both of which in times past have claimed their victims. I wonder when the District Council will recognise the danger of out- door bathing under the conditions at present exist- ing and carry out the promises they made some time ago, for bringing into operation the public baths and washhou.ses Act. There is plenty ot spare ground in the town,and I don't think the ratepayers would grumble if the Council would seriously con- sider the idea of erecting swimming baths. The art of swimming could then be learned without danger, and the risks presented by outdoor bathing would be considerably reduced.</p>
        <p>I must again touch upon the cycling question, and refer to the remarks made by a local writer last week. Firstly I must accept his apology, and I hope to number him as one of my sympathisers. I am pleased to find he knows what a cycle parade is, and as he owns to having been actively connected with one, trust he will come forward and assist in inaugurating a demonstration in Earlestown. I am convinced a lantern parade in the autumn would be a success, and I have not the slightest doubt that the many cyclists in the neighbourhood would willingly assist by their presence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>VARRINGTo.,COUNTI CuURT.</head>
        <p>VARRINGTo.,COUNTI CuURT.</p>
        <p>TIIIIRSDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne-Ffoulkes CLAIM AGAINST A NEWTON Preercerr.—Ralph Johnsen. cabinet maker and upholsterer, Warrington, claimed from John William Webster, licensee of the Millstone, Inn,Newton,£7 15s. 61., for veterinary attendance upon and physic fer his horse in consequence of injuries it had sustained by being wilfully driven into on the high road between VVinwick and Warrington on the 24th February, and for horse hire by plaintiff during the time his own horse was unfit to be worked in conse- quence of the injuries it received. Mr. Fowler (Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.) represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Stuart, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the defend- ant.—Mr. Fowler stated that on the 24th Feoruary Mr. Johnson was returning to Warrington from earlestown in his own dogcart in company with his groom and a workman.. All went well until he approached Warring- ton—just after crossing the borough boundary—when he saw in front of him a governess cart with four men in it. It was being driven from one side of the road to the other. As he neared it the defendant's cart was driven to the wrong aide of the road and a collision took place. The shaft of the defend- ant's cart was driven into the shoulder of the plaintiff's pony and the shock threw one of the occupants of the trap out,and nearly knocked the horse down. Mr. Johnson took bold of the bridle of the de- fendant's horse, and asked for the owner's name. The defendant replied " The Duke of York," and at the same time struck the horso's head with the reins and endea- voured to get it away. The plaintiff stuck to the reins of the defendant's horse, and the defendant then gave his name as Thompson. The plaintiff asked his groom to hold the defendant's horse whilst he went for a police officer. Mr. Johnson went to Warrington in search of a constable,and on returning with P.C. Barnett he met the defendant and others coming in the direction of War- rington. The defendant gave his name to the police officer. The plaintiff had not claimed for injury to the horse.but for veterinary attendance which amounted to £5 ss. Bd., and £4 10e. for horse hire during its illness. —Ralph Jolanson,the plaintiff,corroborated his solicitor's statement. He was standing with his dogcart close to the edge of the parapet on his right side when the col- lision occurred. He admitted that he pulled his pony a little across the road, out that was in order to prevent the pony being struck in the cheat. The shaft of the defendant's cart entered the shoulder of witness's pony and went to a depth if about nine inches. The defendant was not drunk but he had had some drink. There was no excuse for the defendant driving into his (witness's) pony.—Alfred Brandridge, groom to the plaintiff, also gave evidence. It was a clear night, and they could see the defendant's cart 100 yards in front of them. The defendant was driving front one side of the road to the other. Witness shouted to them, but they were talking so loud to one another that he did not think they heard him. Mr. Johnson was on his right side of the road, and when the collision seemed immin- ent, he pulled up close to the kerbstone. Witness held the defendant's horse whilst Mr Johnson went for a policeman. During that time one of the men in defend- ant's:cart assaulted witness.—Edmund Barnton, cabinet maker at Mr. Johnson'i works, deposed that he was in the trap with Mr. Johnson at the time of the collis- ion. He was knocked out of the trap by the force of the impact. Mr. Johnson was drivinz on his right side. —H. Fergusson, veterinary surgeon, stated that he attended the injured pony belonging to the plaintiff. There was a wound between the shoulder blade and the ribs which penetrated about nine inches. There was another slight inquiry about the tenth rib. The shaft seemed to have entered at an angle of about 45 degrees. He attended the horse until about the end of March. The amount claimed for witness's services was the same as he charged.—Mr. Stuart for the defendant, said his client weuki repudiate the state- ments which had been made by the witnesses that he ever gave such a foolish name as the" Duke of York." —His Honour : It is quite immaterial. What does that signify ?—Mr. Stuart said it had been brought up so often. Continuing, be said the statements made by the plaintiff's witnesses were totally contrary to what his client told him were the facts. The defendant and the three other occupants of the governess cart had been to a cattle sale at Warrington. On returning home to New- ton, they saw the plaintiff's trap, just after they had passed a brewer's dray. The occupants of the cart both whistled and shouted to the plaintiff to get out of the mat —His Honour: Why didn't the defendant keep on the right side of the road ?—Mr. Stuart : He did keep on his own side, and the accident did not happen in the way suggested by the plaintiff. The plaintiff him- self admitted in cross-examination that he. pulled his pony across the road, 'it was not for him to judge what was going to happen. He could not tell whether the shafts would strike his animal or not, and by pulling his pony across the road in the way the plaintiff did,he contributed to the accident—HlS Honour said he should not go into the question of c mtributory negligence.— Mr. Stuart said he was sorry his Honour would not, but his client contended that he was on the right side ef the road. The collision was a pure accident. With reference to the claim far horse hire, no evidence had been given that it was necessary tor the plaintiff to engage another horse or that he did engage one. He (Mr. Stuart) sub- mitted that the plaintiff could not recover the £4 7s. he claimed for horse hire.—John William Webster, publi- can. Millstone Inn, Newton. stated that he was on the right side of the road when the collision happened. It was dark at the time, and he could not see many yards in front of him. His (witness's) horse had been in- jured by the accident. He never knew that plaintiff's horse was injured until he received a letter claiming damages. The plaintiff :never said that his horse was hurt. The shafts of his vehicle bent downwards at the end.—Robert Hibbert, butcher, Newton, stated that he was in company with the defendant at the time of the accident. It was dark at the time. They were travel- ling on the right side of the road. He did not know that the plaintiff's pony was injured at the time of the colli- sion.—ln cross-eeamination, witness said they had only had two glasses of bitter beer each during the day. At this point, his Honour said the case would be adjourned until the next court.</p>
        <p>#l4 pecul!ar action was heard by his Honour Judge Junes at the Bolton County Court on Wednesday. Through her father, a child. • named Alice Ann Lowe claimed £5O from Robert Walsh, slater, Nile-street. Walsh. it aPPearB had met two friend is, in whose corn- ' pany he com,urned two glasses of whisky. Being unac- customed to the stimulant, t affected him considerably, and in going home he staggered and fell upon the breaking her right leg. The little one was only three years old. Aedical evidence went to show that the girl would be crippled for life, having a short leg. There was no denial on the part of the defendant, who expressed sorrow at the occurreuce.—The Judge, seeing that the injury was not caused wilfully, gave a verdict for £4O, granting the parents £lO for expenses and ordering the remaining £3O to remain in court for the girl.</p>
        <p>TRWELLING TRUNKS.—G 4RIAND, Ironmonger, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIA.NIENT. FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>The Piling MINISTER made a statement of great im- portance in the douse of Lords on Friday evening with regard to British policy in the Soudan. The noble Marquis began by stating that the Government had been of opinion for some time that it would be neces- sary to recover for Egypt the territory lost in 1882 and 1884. The movement was hastened by the peril iu which the Italians at Rascals were placed. Ells Lord- ship declared that Dongola was the objf,ctive at pre•ent, but the Cabinet had" looked beyond "that point. They were limiting the advance to Eongola at present, how- ever, because there was no money at the disposal of the Egyptian Government to provide for the cost of a further advance southward. He declared, however, that in his opinion" we shall never have restored Egypt to the position in which we received her, and that we shall not have placed Egypt in the poaition of safety in which she desires to stand, until the Egyptian flag floats over Khartoum."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Mr. CueloN, in reply to Sir C. Dilke,said the negotiations with France with rference to Madsgasca.r were bling continued,but as it was a matter of considerable importance it could not be hurried.</p>
        <p>Asked by Mr. C. P. SCOTT whether he would cause the military censorship on telegrams from the Sludan to be suspended with a view to testing the accuracy of the statements of a special correspondent with regard to the looting of the property of the dervishes and the "trium- phant appropriation" of their women by the back troops alter the battle of Firket, Mr. CIItiZON said he saw no necessity for the suspension of the censorship, and he did not think that the words qulted demanded the construction that appeared Le have suggested itself in some quarters, When the House went into Committee on the Eiuca- tion Bill, an amendment was moved by Mr. S. EvANS, with the object of alto wing the council of every urkan district with a population of over twenty thousand to appoint an educational authority, was rejected by 265 votes to 143, and a proposal by Mr. T. CI. Bowles, that every council of a municipal borough which was also a Parliamentary borough should be allowed to appoint an educational authority, was defeated by 281 votes to 128. The committee was still on the first line of the bill when progress was reported.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords Lord RIPON called the attention of the Government to the plrdge they had given to in- stitute an inquiry with regard to the Jameson raid, and asked what steps had been taken. Lord Selborne said the Government were not disposed to open the inquiry until the trial of Dr. Jameson and his officers was com- pleted. In the course of further discus4on Lord Salis- bury said that the criminal inquiry must first be con- ducted exclusively by the tribuuals to which it Wal con- fided. It would be inconvenient, as well as unjust, to have anything like a parallel examination before a judi- cial tribunal and a committee of one of the Houses of Parliament. Lord Rosebery said there was no wiTh to prejudice the trial of Dr. Jameson. but the Prime Minister must acknowledge that until this matter was exhaustively sifted the directors of the Chartered Com- pany, the Ministry, and the country itself would be under an imputation difficult to remove. Any other Government would have taken the earliest opportune y to justify themselves before their own people and the world against any such suspicion. If ever there was a travesty and a mockery of inquiry it was this. They all felt that until this inquiry was held they would lie under some imputation, and yet they were to stay their hand and say : " Let us first try Dr. Jameson ; then let the Cape Parliament's inquiry take place ; and when they have reported and all is stale and flyblown our inquiry is to take place."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday Mr. CARVELL WILLIAMS asked the Vice President of the Committee of Council on Education if he would eKplain on what grounds the department had replied to the Nonconform- ist parents of 193 children in Heywood who memorial- ised them for free places in a Public elementary school that there were at present 296 free places available in the district in which such children resiiied, and that therefore there was a sufficient supply of free school accommodation; whether he was aware that 172 of such places were in a Church of England school and 10J in a Roman Catholic school ; and whether the Department had decided that the accommodation in such schools was suitable for the children ot Nonconformists. Sir J. GORST replied that the facts were correctly stated, and the answer to the last paragraph was in the affirma- tive. _ _</p>
        <p>_..„. The House afterwards went into Committee on the Education Bill, resuming the consideration of an amend- ment moved by Sir J. Lubbock the objee. of which was that in places where the areas of the School Board and the county or borough council 'were conterminous the education authority should be a joint committee of the two, with, the mover of the amendment hoped, sonic members added to represent the principal educational institutions. At the close of a prolonged debate Mr. BALFOUR moved the closure. The Chairman a,cepted the motion, which was carr ed by 219 votes to 134, and Sir J. Lubbock's amendment was then rejected by. 212 votes to lig. Mr. BRYNMOR Jomts moved to insert the word " may" instead of " shall," so that the clause would read " every c3uncil may appoint an education authority for the purposes of this Act." This was defeated by 250 votes to 139, and the Committee were discussing an amendment proposed by Sir Charles Dilke, to the effect that the county council in aispointing an education authority should have due regard to the representation of minorities, when progress was reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons on Wednesday, in committee on the Education Bill, resumed consideration on the amendment proposed by Sir Charles Dilke providing that in appointing the education committees the County Councils should have due regard to the representation of minorities. Sir JOHN GORST said the Government entirely sympathised with the object of the amendment, but the plan of the Bill was to leave all such matters to the discretion of the County Councils themselves. There was only one exception which the Government were in- clined to make,and that was in favour of Mr. Jebb's amendment suggesting that the County Councils sllouTcl take into consideration the inclusion in the education committees of persons, not being members of the Coun cil, who had special educational knowledge and experi- ence. Mr. ATRF.RLF.Y-JONES proposed that the county or muni- cipal couneil itself should be the education authority. Sir J. GORST opposed the proposal, which he said was practically a new scheme. Subsequently, in answer to Mr. J. Stuart,Sir J. GORST said that in respect to finance the eiucation committee would3be entirely under the county council. The general position of the committee could be best illustrated by saying that it was intended to have the same pasition in relation to the county coun- cil that the watch committee in a municipal borough had with regard to the municipal c3uncil. Mr. AFQUITII said Sir John Gorses etatement had entirely changed the situation. They now learned for t,e first time that the committee to be appointed was to be a sovereign authority. He declared unhesitatingly that the commit- tee must be subordinate to the elected representatives of the ratepayers, and that they viere not going to entrust the powers given under the Bill to a body that was to a large extent co-opted. Sir W. HARCOURT and Mr. Accalsn followed to the same effect. Mr. BALFOUR said that if members desired at a later stage to introduce any terms as to the education committee reporting to the county council they could do so. On a division the amendment was defeated by 293 votes to 118. The discussion was then adjourned, and the ['MUSA rose. HORSDA.Y.</p>
        <p>Asked by Mr. T. BAYLEY in the House of Cimmone On Thursday if he could furnish the number of natives shot and hanged as spies in Bulawayo and the neighbour- hood, and whether any form of trial took place before the execution, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he was unable to say. He had, h )wever, no doubt that any spies taken were tried by some form of court-martial. A little later while answering a question put by Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said he had received a telegram stating that Sir F. Carrington had issued a general order in which he directed that during the continuance of hos- tilities clemency was to be shown to the wounded, that women and children were not to be injured, and that prisoners were to be taken wherever possible. Mr. G. CURZON, replying to Mr. J. Ellis, said the British Consul in Crete had reported that three churches had been desecrated by Turkish soldiers. The repre- sentatives of the Great Powers were in conatant com- munication, and their combined efforts were being " en- ergetically directed to induce the Turkish Government te take steps of a conciliatory character with a view to restoring order and preventing further confUcts." In answer to Mr. S. Smith, who asked whether it was true that Venezuelan soldiers had invaded British Guiana, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said the Government had received a telegram stating that some Venezuelan soldier 3 had crossed the boundary and had interfered with the pro- ceedings of a body of English officials who were survey- ing a road. The House again went into Committee on the Educa- tion Bill. Mr. J. STUART moved an amendment with the object of excluding elementary education from the purview of the proposed authority. After a prolonged debates motion to repot.' progress was rejected by 254 votes to 131. Mr. BALFOUR then moved the closure, which was carried by 256 votes to 142, and Mr. Stuart's amendment was subsequently defeated by 258 votes to 131. The debate was then adjourned.</p>
        <p>---.-..1111,1..--.</p>
        <p>THE INDEPENDENT -AIRTHODTST CHAPEL,</p>
        <p>C RAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>_ On Monday evening at toe above place of worship Mr. J. Whitley, Stockton Heath, delivered a very interesting lecture on" Epitaphs." The chair was taken by Mr. J. Mort, of Lymm, and there was a good attendance. Mr. Whitley's lecture occupied over an hour,and as he has been collecting epitaphs for twenty years, he was able to put before his bearers a most complete discourse on that subject. Needless to say the lecturer was attentively listened to, and at the close a vote of thanks to himself and the chairman was passed. A collection was taken on behalf of the debt on the building, and it rea- lised a satisfactory amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, JUNE 20, 1996.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, JUNE 20, 1996.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED CASE OF SWINDLINU</p>
        <p>Ar WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>TERRIBLE SHIPPING DISASTER. mu.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE SHIPPING DISASTER. mu.</p>
        <p>Continued from page 3.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon two important telegrams which throw some light at least upon the cause of the casuality, were received at the Company's office in Fen- church-st. The first was dated June 18, 1.20 p.m. It was in French, and when translated read as follows : Loss of Drummond Castle occasioned by rain and fog. Toe names of the three men saved are Charles Wood, quartermaster; James Godbolt, sailor ; and Charles Marquardt,passenger. Since last evening .18 bodies have been rocoverei at Molene, and the body of a woman at Ushant. The French naval authorities sent a steamer to the scene ot the disaster yesterday.—KEßßOS." The second telegram was from the British Consul at Brest, and read as follows :—" Names of the saved at Molene : Charles Wood, quarter- master, aad William James Godbolt, seaman. Both arrived here this morning, well. Name of survivor at UsLant, Charles Marquardt. The body at Molene is that of Mr. Herbert Heinds (Hinds ?). Three only at this hour known to be saved. According to Wood and Godbolt, the steamer struck betweeh 10.30 and 11.30 o'clock; the captain and Third Officer Brown' on the bridge then. The weather not foggy, but drizzly rain. They will send their statements to the Board of Trade as soon as possible. They attribute casualty to hazy weather. No lights seen. Master took soundings two hours before she struck. Marquardt is weak, but will come to Brest.—CoNsuL." THE VICTIMS: INTERESTING DETAILS.</p>
        <p>The greatest sufferers by the calamity are a Cornish family of the name of Peachey, the head of whom was Mr. Thomas Peachey, who went to the Cape as one of the earliest pioneers, 46 years ago, and who, with his relatives, sixteen in all, was on his way to England for a six months' holiday,having been invited to West Corn- wall by Mr. Millett, of Venage. Lelant, who was him- self a former settler with Mr. Peachey. Mr. Go'irge Hinds, mentioned in the list, is stated to be a Carlisle man. He emigrated and became a success- ful tea planter, and married a daughter of Mr. Peachey. Mr. John Cornforth, who joined the Drummond Castle at LAS Palmas,was a son ot the late Mr. Cornforth, ship- owner, West Hartlpool ; and Mr. Harry Wilson,who em- barked at toe same place,was also a Hartlepool man, be- ing a son of Mr. Councillor J. F. Wilson, shipowner. Mr. J. Wayman, who was second in command to Captain Pierce, lived when at home at Talbct- ville, Bournemouth, where he married a few years ago, and leaves a widow and two young children. Mrs. Wayrnan was recently informed by the o..mipany that the Drummond Caste, owing to the pressure of business at the Cape, had been delayed for two days, but from a letter received en Wednesday she was led to expect her husband home on Saturday, the vessel being timed to arrive in London not later than that day.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Barnet, who, with her daughter, has perished, was born at Tickhill, Yorkshire. She had been living at Johannesburg, and waS visiting England after being away for twelve years. Her daughter was a child aged eight. Mr. Gethim, who, with his wife and two tiaughters, was amongst the lost, was an architect at Cardiff. Mrs. Gethitra was a daughter of Mr. T. R. Thompson, one of the directors of the Barry Railway Company. The family were accompanied by a governess and a nurse, Mrs. Gethim being in delicate health. The family left England in September last, and they were to have been welcomed home by Mr. Thompson and family,who were waiting in London to meet them on their return. A singularly sad case is that of Mr. William Roberts, who wai on his way to Wales to marry Miss Evans,who is employed in the telegraph department of the Cardiff Post-office. Mr. Roberts intended to take his bride back to the Cape. Sae read an account of the disaster in an evening paper, and she at once wired to the Company, whose reply led her to fear the worst. One of the ship's company, named Thompson, was returning to England after an absence of four years. In a letter addressed to his mother he promised to settle down and marry a young woman residing at Battersea. His intended bride, on hearing of the loss of the ship, was suddenly taken ill and died. -Miss Brinkley was glister to Captain Brinkley, chief constable of Warwickshire. She was returning from Capetown. Captain Brinkley left for the scene of the disaster on Thursda y. Mr. Cadbury was Mr. Richard Cadbury, jun., of Bir- mingham. MISd Peace and nurse,Mrs. M'Lean and four children, Mrs. Morton and two children, and Mrs. Andrew had friends in Glasgow. Miss Peace was inquired for by Professor Myth, of the technical College. Mrs. Hugh MeLean and children were inquired for by a sister-in- law at 62, Efarvie-at., Bridgeton. She was on her way home from Johannesburg._ Mrs. Andrew who was en- _</p>
        <p>quired for by Mr. -W. Andrew, Great Western Road, was returning home with her four-months-old child, having lost her husband in South Africa. She was the wife of Thomas Andrew, who was the manager of a distillery near Pretoria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LANCASHIRE PASSENGERS LOST.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE PASSENGERS LOST.</p>
        <p>Several Lancashire passengers lost their lives on the boat. The Rev. F. B. Sandbach, of Stockport (brother of Mr. Sandbach, wine merchant, of St. Ann's-square, Manchester), and Mrs. Sandbach were returning from the Canaries. Until about two years ago Mr. Sandbach was a Wesleyan minister at Moseley, Birmingham. He had since been stationed in the Stockport circuit at , Tiviotdale. He went to the Canaries because of the state of his health. Atiother victim is Mr. Fred Bradshaw engaged with the firm of Mossrs. R. B. Carr, Lomas,and Co., of Meal-st., Manchester. Mr. Bradshaw lived at Southport. He joined the Drummond Castle at Las Palmas,where he had gone to recruit his health. He Wa'S well known in athletic circles. Among the p issengers from Capetown were two daughters ot Mr. Olive, who until a recent date held an important post in the office of the City Surveyor of Manchester, and who is now chief of the same department under the Corporation of Capetown. The young , ladies were on their way to a school at Llandudno. There were also ameng the passengers Walter Whipp ' and Fred Whipp, cotton spinners and manufacturers. Clithero.l. Fred took an active part in the cotton busi- ness, ani some years ago he visited Australia, after- wards publishing his experiences. He was a prominent member of the Technical Instruction Committee for the borough. Walter had a reputation as a breeder and exhibitor of shire horses. Both were widely known. Walter's health broke down a year ago, and, accompanied by his brother, he left England for South Africa. The object of the journey had been accom- plished, and the two were returning. The firm owns mills in other Lancashire towns. Recent letters from the two travellers showed that they had had some excit- ing experienct's at Johannesburg and Bulawayo recently. On Thursday the Town Council passed a vote of condol- ence with the familv,the Mayor stating that the botough had lost two useful and prominent townsmen. Messrs. Whipp 'originally intended remaining a short time longer in South Africa, but a desire was expressed that they hould curtail their visit, and they did so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>DEITII OF LORD LILFORD.</head>
        <p>DEITII OF LORD LILFORD.</p>
        <p>Lordlford died on Wednesday afternoon at his Northamptonshire seat, at Lilford Hall, near 0 unci le, at the age af 63. Three weeks since he caught a chill and serious complications followed, ending in his death. Ho is succeeded as Baron Lilford by his eliest surviving son, the Hon. John Powys. Lord Lilford was one of the leading authorities on British birds,and possessed one of the largest private collections in Englaud. Deceased was for many years president of the Northamptonshire Natural History Society, and in the park at Lilford had probably the finest existing collection of birds. The deceased gentleman was not known except by repute in Warrington, as he never visited the town, but amongst Churchmen his name was a household word. Ile was an extensive owner of property in the district, and was a generous benefactor to many local institutions,especially those of an educational character. He contributed to the support of the many organizations of the parish of St. Elphin's, Warrington, when-. his death will be deeply regretted. That his lordship did good very unosteutatiously is showu by a circumstance which took place in Warrington some years ago. While a fund was being raised for the building of St. Luke's Church a printed circular asking for pecuniary assist- ance was sent to the deceased as well as to others. No special appeal was made to Lord Lilford, but per return of post Mr. Carleten received a checoe for £3OO. It may be remembered that the' Hon. John Powys opened tee bazaar held some time ago at Parr in con- nection with the Parish Church. • The London cocrespendent of the Manchester" Guar- dian "says :—By the death of Lord Lilford English zoological science loses one of its foremost supporters. Although he has been an invalid for the past few years, he maintained up to the last his love aPil interest for the study of zoology. Indeed I happen to know that only a few months before his death his anxiety that the Euro- pean fauna should be adequately represented in the , national collections led Lord Lilford to employ at his own expense collectors in different parts of Europe for the special purpose of obtaining specimens and material for • an exhaustive work on the subject. His fear, often ex- pressed,was that before long many of the forms CMHITIOD enough now would disappear from the face of the earth without any proper effort having been made to . preserve a series of specimens for all time. Although Lord Lilford has not lived te see the completion of his great work on the birds of the Britith Islands.commenced in 1885, it is satisfactory to note that thirty-two parts have been issued, and that only three parts are required to finish it. He was president of the British Ornitho- logists' Union, in which capacity he rendered great ser- vice tritscience. It was characteristic of the can that he could not be prevailed upon to join the British Orni- thologists' Club, which was fermed a few years age as a kind of inner circle (.1 the larger body.</p>
        <p>'IRE PUBLIC ARE W 411 NED against the use of Foreign c,:oas contaming alali, producing high'y coloured prcparA- Lions offered as ' pure eocoa " Cadbury's Cocos is absolutply pare and unadulterated, and is thk refore the best. The " Lancet" describes it as " the standard of highest purity at present attainable,"—ildvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ADJOURNED HEARING.</head>
        <p>ADJOURNED HEARING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning at the Warrington Police Court, before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair), Mr. J. W. HAS:- ward, and Mr. S. Wright, John Grant alias Garlick,alias Dickson was charged, on remand, with obtaining by false pretences on the 4th May, the sum of £5O from Margaret Ann Ashmore ;he was also charged with ob- taining the sum of £6O from William Stockton, on.the 17ch of April, also by false pretences. Mr. Browne again appeared to prosecute and Mr. F. R. Roberts defended.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne made a lengthy statement to the bench, reviewing the facts of the case, so far as he knew them. lie asked their worships, when they had heard the evid- ence he would put forward, to commit prisoner to trio Liverpool Quarter Sessions to take his trial. The first witness called was _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr: Gilbert,manager of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank, who said prisoner opened an account at the bank on the 17th April and closed it on May 11th. He had nothing in the bank on April 13th. Witness pre- duceer a copy of the account. On the 17th of Aoril £64 was paid in to his account. Oa the 4.h of May £5O was paid in. Including those two sums his whole cred,t was £132. On the 9th of M y there was a payment to pri- soner of £4O. On May 11 he drew out £1 53. In cross-examination by Mr. Roberts wttnes3 said the £1 si. was paid to a p-rson named Wilcick. The £4O was the only amount paid out to self, and that was on May 9th, a Saturday. Prisoner had no overdraft, and never asked for one. Mr. William Isaac, grocer, 26, Buttermarket-street, Warrington, said he had no contract with prisoner for the supply of £6o' worth of boxes a month. He had given him an order for ten gross of bnxes,which would come to about £24. He only supplied £5 worth and they were not up to the mark. Mr. A. B. Kenworthy, assistant to Mr. James Ken- worthy. Warrington,said they stopped supplying timber to prisoner on the 24th March. On that date about £6O was owing by tile prisoner. They had receivsd £8 on account since. On the 12th of May they caused a writ to be issued for the remainder of the money. Cross-examined : He knew that Mr. Kenworthy offered to accept £5 a week. Mr. Robert Lowe, accountant, &amp;en, said he saw Grant in January last, and he told witness that he had £2OO and wished to take a works. Be wanted to know if he had any to let. He subsequently took the works in Lythgoes-lene. Oa the 12 n May he had business with the prisoner about insurance. He asked if witness knew anything about deeds of assignment,and witness told him he did. He then handed to witness the whole of his creditors' vouchers, and they went tnrough the day. brink together. Witness at his request marked off all the accounts that had been paid. He told witness Cal&amp; well's had isseed a writ due that day and that, he ex- pected a sheriff's our •er. Be returned to the works and told Mr. Grant that he had learned that the manager for Caldwell's had gone to Wigan to bring the cfficer. The workmen would not let him go as they panted their wages. Witness eventually arranged ta go for prisoner to Mr. Roberts' office to execute the deed of assignment while prisoner went to the bank to cash a cheque of £5 for a man named Mercer. Witness went to Mr. Roberts' office bet the prisoner did not turn up. According to the v-uchers handed to witness on the 17th April by prisoner his liabilities then amounted to £522 53. 2d. On May they were £BO5 164. 51. according to the same f'vhnoirf chers. No rent had been paid to his know. ledge. Most of the machinery in the works belonged to the landlord. The only machinery sold by the eherriff's officer was one lathe. By Mr. Roberts : The bulk of the money owed woirld be due. On one occasion some of the men tried to Itenp him there to pay wages. He thought the men would have kept him there. He had prisoner's cash hook and wa(eeB slip. He did not know that Grant re- fused to execute a deed of assignment. He rather hesi- tated. He did not say he could pay 203. in the £. Re-ex ,mined by Mr. Browne witness said on the 2nd May there were £92 173. 81 on the wrong side of the cash hock. The largest payment he had received from any person was £5 from Isaac's. Ho had no receipts for any large amounts paid by prisoner except one for £lO for eareiaee of goods on the M.S. and L.R. There were 35 or 36 tranea.ctions which had passed through the day hook. They were from March 9-h to May Bth. The total outstanding debts were £52 17s. id. and most were dignilted. William Stockton, 54, Forshaw-street, Warrington, said in April last he was out of work and went to gee defendant on the 13th April. He had previously seen advertisements in the papers asking for a partner. When he saw prisoner he asked him if he could find him employment. Prisoner said " Yes." if he was open to invest any money. He said he W3.i doing a good trade there and could get plenty of men hut he wanted someone with capital. He took witness through the works and there appeared to be plenty going on. Prisoner showed witness his stock, which ho said was all his own, and said he would not allow witness to and dd Yorkshire child. took witness in saidbhl nakhp :dhnas'dlrs moneyalga hainho: Lancashirewthxse:Pntonhei.s wHwife the warehouse where they Were making flour boxes and said he trul a contract with Mr. William Isaac for £6O a month. Witness had no previous knowledge of what boxes cnst. Prisoner eeked him how much he had to invest and witness told him £6O. They werlenhaitslsswaver- inrrs at the" Linn " He asked prisoner if he invested tt ho ea meinuneeryv wa, Itr 01 ha w was jest the men to suit him. He promised witness 303. thing that is clean ahri'fidtn dhrelelscinmoocu ltde:bdploelo.'w3: initPnerenistiosnaeird s a"i da nhy; a week and Vt nor cent. per annum on his money. Witness told him he ernarld ennsider it. Prisoner said he would make R. A. Navinr sit up that summer. Witness knew Mr. Naylor employed hundreds of men. Witness and prisoner lived in the same street and he told prisoner that eenther gentleman wished him to go into partner- ship Grant suggested that he (himself) should go and see this gentleman and advien him what to do. Prisoner saw him again and advised him to have nothing to do with the gentleman. Fle told witneee he could start work on the rollowinz W ,rlnesi,r. On the 14th April he gent him a note prnmieing him work at 30g. a week for 561 hours in return for the investment of the £6O. He paid prisoner the £6O on Friday, the 17th April. about 2 45. Prisoner wrote out the agreement producel. Witness contintred work until the 25th April. He re-1 ceived one week' wags. Prisoner refused to pay him the wages for three dere he had worked. Prisoner told him on the day he ne!d him that he need not start until he eaw'him on the Monday. He said he wanted to see an interested party on the Monday. and asked him to wait until he had seen that party. On the 28th April he saw him again and he said he had not come to terms with the" party " When witness went on the Thursday a tremendous quantity of gond.; had disappeared, includ- ing a lot of the new tents. On the let May witness issued a warrant for false nreteneee against prigoner. He gave witness a charge on corns furniture deposited with Mt. Mainwaring. This was found to be wnrthlese. If the prisoner had not told him he had money in the bank, witness would not have advanced the £BO. Neither would he if he hail not been premised a permanent en- gagement at 30e. n week, or if he knew that prisoner owed what he did at the time. Coupled with these, he would not have done so had he known prisoner had no Contract with Mr. Isaac. By Mr. Rnherts witness said he first saw prisoner in February, about three months before the engagement. Something was said then shout comment...int; a firewood business. Bees use it was not an established begin, es he refugee] to go on with it. He knew prisoner was in the town carrying en the bueineee. Prisoner told him he had money in February. In April he said he was short I of eanital. He aid net ask Seeksr (Grant's manager) about the business.- Fre did not ask prisoner for refer- ences. Prisoner did not tell him that he wasted to open ant *cement. He did riot know that prisoner had not anr banking: aCroUnt. He did not know that certain birainesa debts were paid by Grant out of the money., When he was at the wn he two at three days and was etaeking timher.prienrer told him and another man that he could get men ten an their work for 18s. a week. Wit- ness was nsrpn9ihiv engaged 'to book everything that came in and everything that went out. He left the " Lien "because of the new management. Me waernot diemissei for misconduct. When asked to wait by Grant.he did not infer that he Was dismissed. There were between 20 and 30 people thereeinme of them girls, and their mothers came to blackguard Grant for their money. (Laughter.) Mr. C. Lynn, branch manager of the" Liverpool Daily Pest "and " Echo," produced papers containing adver- tisements given by prisoner to him. One for the 29th of April was paid six days following. Prisoner a Ivey- tised neder at last e nemeo,—Grant, Quebec " and "Finance." appleine for partners, Orrice,' &amp;c., and asking for the loan of £2OO. On Anril 10'h there were two advertisements which Mr. Grant paid for. Another advertisement anplyine for travellers was signed Seeker, care of T. Grant. dripping refinery, Lethenee-lene, Warrington. Another was " ynnth, with respectable horse and cart, wanted—(laughter),--apply to Seeker, &amp;re" By Mr. Roberts : It was not an unusual thine for one person to advertise under various nom-de-plumes. Other advertisements were inserted for ordinary workmen. Money was not mentioned. Mr.Ftroevne here said as Mre.A,himore's evidence would he very lengthy it was suggested that the case he re- manded Fore week, Mr. Roberts agreed to this course, and it was adopted.</p>
        <p>The final stage in the proceedings at Bow-at.. against Dr. Jameson and his offieors was reached on Monday. The Attnrney General asked that the six defendants should tr cQmmittert for trial and the remaining, nine diFcharr,ed, and Sir E Clark offered nn rponsitinn. Sr John Bridge then formally committed Dr. Jameson, Sir J. Willoughby, Hon F Whit., Colonel Grey, Hon. H. White and Major Coventry ; the others wer ischarged.</p>
        <p>The Gr.nd Jurr t Be-I,shire ASFi7,Pq nn Wednegeay threw nut the Bill which charged Marc Ann Palmer with aiding her mother, the notorious Mrs. Dyer, in murder,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Still another victory must be added to the already long list of successes which have rewarded the efforts of the Warrington eleven this season. Only once this year have they tasted defeat, and the present season promises to be one of the most successful—if not the most success- ful—in the history of the club. Saturday's victory was the secand they have gained at the expense of the Brock- lands team this season. The first match was played at Warrington and ended in the decisive defeat of the visiting team. With the object of making the return engagement a little more mustardly, the Brooklands men whipped together a strong eleven, but their batsmen made but a poor show before the deliveries of Connor and Scrimshaw, and the result was a brilliant victory for Warrington by no fewer than 126 runs.</p>
        <p>The chief absentee from the Warrington eleven was Mr. J. J. Crosfield who, at the last moment, was unable to journey with the team. This was quite unexpected, and as there was no time to fill the vacancy, the team was obliged to go with ten men. A substitute was found at Brooklands, and he creditably filled the vacancy. The home captain (T. P. Bellhouse) won the toss, and decided that his men should be the first to go to the wickets. The initial batsmen were the brothers Hartley, but the score had only reaehed 14 when Scrimshaw disturbed C. Hartley's wickets with a well-delivered ball. Lomas was the succeeding bats- man, and with his first stroke he scored four by a snick to the boundary. The score had been augmeuted to 22 when Connor made a rensarkable performance by taking three wickets in three successive balls, or in the usual cricket phraseology, accomplishing the "hat trick." His first victim was A. Hartley, who gave a catch to Hampson which that player successfully negotiated. Connor's next ball dismissed Clay, and with an extra fast one, W. B. Steel was completely bafil-d, and his wickets were scattered. This sensational performance on a wicket which was not one of the best from a bowler's point of view, was received with a ringing cheer by the spectators.</p>
        <p>Further disaster attended the home team, for in the next over Lomas was howlf.d by Scrimshaw. The score otill stood at 22. and half the home side were out. The captain tried to stem the tide of disaster by forcing the pace a little. But the excel- lent bowling and fielding of the visiting team made un- etting very difficult. Connor's bowling proved most deadly and in his fifth over he dismissed Davies. Ireland commenced confidently, but when six had been added to his ~redit,Scrimshaw knocked his wickets down. Pagden and Bel!house had similar experiences immediately afterwards so that when the last two men were a: the wickets only 39 had been scored. A little • slogging" increased the total to 49 before the Brooklands' inninea was brought to a close by the downfall of Cart's wickets.</p>
        <p>Of the ten men who had been dismissed, nine of them were clean bowled by either Connor or Scrim- shaw. These two players bowled unchanged.and the shortness of the home team's innings may be judged by the fact that eleven overs by Cinnor and ten by Scrim- shaw sufficed to dismiss the batsmen. Scrimshaw had the heat average with five wickets for 19 runs, and Con- nor ran him closely with five for 26 runs.</p>
        <p>The Warrington innings commenced with Thewlis and Callon and both batsmen severely punished the bowling. The home team's score was passed in quick time without the loss of a single wicket. Several bowlers were tried but the score hid reached 65 before Callon was finely caunht by Lomas. Connor gave no trouble, hut when G. Clarke and Thewlis became partners runs were scorei rapidly. Cheers were given when the century was hoisted, and were renewed when the telegraph board chewed that Thewlis had contributed 50. The visiting clptain was caught nut when four more runs had been added, but in AC. Nelson, Thewlis found another good partner. The score had reached 133 before Thewlis was bowled by Clay. His inninge was a brilliant display, and beyond one "chance," his 61 was compiled in grand style. Of the remaining Warrington batsmen. Nelson and Podreore were the only two to reach double figures, and the innings closed for 175 This left the substantial margin of 126 runs in favour of the visiting team.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between Warrington and Brook- lands, second elevens.was played at Arpley, on Saturday As the toss went against Warrington.the homeaters bat- ted first and compiled 107 runs before being dismissed. Moir Byres opened their innings with 18. Holgate and Roots, the next two batsmen accnmolished little, but Watson retrieved the position with 57 runs from his bat, Hartley was the only other of the visitnrs to score double figures, his total being 11. When the hnmesters went to the wickets they played with a certain amount of con- fidence and freedom. Unsworth and Conk were the first partners and both hit out freely. The score was just two short of the half century when Cook was caught by Willineat, after having made 24. Unaworth soon followed, with 23 to his credit. Some good scoring followed, for Duckworth contributed 10, Evans and Crompton 17, IBowen 38. and Lowe 16. Thus the scoring on the home side was very fairly distributed instead of being con- fined to one player. On Saturday the two Warrington teams scored almost the same total, viz., the first team 135. and the second 118.</p>
        <p>--- There was another match played in the Warrington rrieket League on ;•Naturday, the ()noosing elevens being Sankey Wire Mills and St. Elphin's. Having the choiee the Sainte sent in the vors, and were beaten to start with if they had only known it. Woodcock and Bailey opened the match exceedingly well. The latter retired with 13, but Wnndward outlasted the match, compiling a level half century and then was not out. But for these two players tha Wire Mille team would have done badly. As muchehowever. can be said of the Saints, who only manared to enmpile 42,0 f which more than half was nut together by Barnes:the winners had a majority of 47,t0 which result the deadly bowling of Simpson and linhqnn largely contributed. Tts e former took seven wickets for 20 runs, and the latter Ithree for 18 runs.</p>
        <p>Runs arpeared hard to Ret at Stockton Heath. on Saturday when the Temperance team engaged Warring- ton St. Luke's. Of the wholp 22 players only one man made anything like a respectable display with the hat, and that was Hallows who was run out after cmmenc- ine the home innings with 11. The devotees of Tem- perance totalled 30 runs while their visitors only made 14.</p>
        <p>Last week I commented upon the value of Ames Monks as a player for the Cheshire Lines Engineers, but apparently there are times when he is hot infallible. On Saturday he did a fair share towards getting out Peter Walker and Sons team for 80. and the other share was done by Harrison. The total did not seem a formidable one and the Engineers might be pardoned for consider. inr their ehannes fairly rombut unfortunately for them the brewery team also possessed a trundler or two of some skill. T. Allen and Todd did not allow a single two- fluured score to b. made against them,and the C.L.E.'s were all out for 29. One run short of this total was contributed for the winners by Ashton, who was the top scorer of the match.</p>
        <p>The juniors of the same two teams also played against one another , and the results were just the opposite,A knocked up 69 runs, against forotnh e E nlnM the Brewery eleven 40 on their own around. Good rice WAR donelor the winners by E Harken 33. and J. Bailey 10. Deeley and Harding dismissed all the homes- ters in (pick style, the highest score made against their bowlinz being 8 by Stone. not out. The Envineers beganas a rune bhad retired with 2,there w of them, and each followed oa badly, fuocrks, , afterfo Du re ee the other. Harding hatched another later on. The Engineers had the majority both in runs and" ducks."</p>
        <p>A tight match was played on the Orford Barracks ffrotind, and which ended in the defeat of thP homesters by 3 rune. The Fairfield eleven were the visitors and between them hatted 51 runs. Bolton made 13, and Welaby and Ellin each 11. Blease for the red-coats scored 14. A determined battle may be expected where British soldiers are concerned and this proved no excep- tion to the rule.</p>
        <p>A team representing Parr's Bank journeyed to High Leh on Saturday, and were, to say the least of it, well wiped ont. AN far as scoring went they did not do b-4dly with a total of 86, but comparatively they did,for 93 was run up by the honesters for only four wickets. Kirby, Platte,. and Garton, for the latter, all exceeded 20,and the last-named carried out his hat. The Bank was hest represented by Broughall and Deane, the former hitting out well for 41, and the latter tor 16.</p>
        <p>Wire met Wire on Saturday when a match was placed between Whitecross and Sankey Wire Mills second, but unlike the case of Greek meeting Greek, when the tussle is supposed to be equally formidable on both Odes. the Whitecross ceam were beaten somewhat easily. Allcock, for Sankey, bowled strongly, and all the White- cross players were out for 51. Their opponents exceeded this score by 14 and still had W. Asnip to bat. No phenomenal scores were made on either side.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Wesleyans received a visit from the Rough Green eleven on Saturday, and beat them by comnilina more than double their total. All the linugh Greeners were out for 27, the bowling being a little too good for them. The homesters totted up 65. A. Barlow w-s by far the most useful player they had, for in addi- tion to scoring 20 runs, not out, he took four wickets for 6 rung. His efforts with the ball were ably supplemented hc Wilkingon, who lowered the bails an equal number of times for 15 runs.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Thomasson, who has for some years been the leader of the Libel. tl Unionists in Bolton, is so dig- satisfied with the policy of the Government, bnth in home and foreizn affairs, that he announces his inability to support them any longer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WARRINGTON PRACTICAL JOKER.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON PRACTICAL JOKER.</p>
        <p>"BEWARE OF THE WILD MAN OF THE WOODS !" The following is one of an excellent series of "Old Stories Re-told "which have appeared in the " Dews- bury Reporter." It will he of interest to Warrington people :—I prepose (says the writer) to devote the pre- sent chapter to Will Dingley,of Warrington,who died up- wards of • wen 4 years ago ; a veterinary surgeon of considerable ability, and who had a strong penchant for practical jokes. Dingley was not his name, but will serve,as his real one will easily be guessed by his towns- people, with many of whom he was a great favourite. My acquaintance with him began about the year 1848, in Warrington fairground, where somehow or other I found ti'e tail of my jacket securely pianed to the shawl of a factory girl standing by ! Our separation wrought some havec with the lassie's attimand I could only ap- pease her wrath by apologising for the misconduct of somebody in a group of a dozen young fellows or more that were laughing heartily at what had occurred. On( amongst them,l was quick to notice, did not even smile, , but was preternaturally solemn ; so solemn that I felt sure he was the culprit, and so it turned out. Dingley was anything but a melancholy-looking chap. I can picture him after all these years. He was tall and , straight, had a merry countenance set off with a pro- fusion of dark curly hair, and was always ready for a bit of fun. A Pecksniffian severeness of aspect ill be- came him when worn as a mask,and could not be main- tained more than a few minutes. The pinning braught us together, like it did myself and the maiden ; but while her disappearance into the heart of the fair terminated our acquaintance, that with Will Dingley lasted many years. Yet we were never intimate. A street-end chat, or a very occasional walk together, that was all ; but he came to the conclusion that I was one to be trusted, and so, without seeking his confidence. I learnt now and then of "good things" that were to sta.rte the town, and as mum was the word before an event, so also was it afterwards, when sometimes the authorities were mak- ing vary diligent inquiries. I should only know of a tithe of his tricks. IF.; last was not confided to me, and I blazoned it forth in the pages of the " Manchester Examiner and Times," withouteof course, giving even a hint at the suspected authorship of what was a very elaborate thing projected and carried out on the lines of the Berners Street hoax, with which the name of Theodore Hook is sometimes associated, this being in 11860,about six months or more before I came to Dews- bury. Now I am not going to write a line in defence of prac- tical joking—it is a poor sort of humour at the best, if there be any humour in it—but on the other hand I don't feel inclined to pen a dietrihe to put it down. even if I could. Says an old saw, " Youth will have its fling," and if youth cannot have that fling in some amusement, even at the occasional expense of others, depend upon it its direction will be still more downward. I am not sure that I should care to make the acquaint- ance of the boy or the girl who at some time or other, and in a pure spirit of mischief, had not perpetrated a few practical jokes. Like the measles, ride old women's tales, they come natural, and there are some hoaxes that descend from generatien to generation, and have quite a long genealogy. I'll be bound to say that Shakespeare as a boy sent a companion or two on a successful quest for strap oil to the nearest cobbler's, and that young Milton, if the truth were known, had induced a friend of like age to himself to fetch some pigeon's milk from the apothecary's. Do you think—l am writing for the bops and girls as well as older folk—that Drake or Frobisher, Churchill (afterwards the Duke of Marlborough) or the iron Duke, Lever had larks at other people's expense when in the days before their teens ? All youngsters sound in 'wind, limb, and eyesight, and bubbling over with merriment, naturally incline to mischief as the needle to the pole, and that mischief often takes the form of practical jok- ing. But such joking is far from being as common as it used to be. In the geed old days, when the streets were guarded, save the mark, by Charleys—semi-decre- pit men whose time for active work was over, and who slept away much of the night in watch-boxes--it was no uncommon thing Nit the young bloods of the period to carry them away, box and all, and once in a way to drop them in a convenient stream. But those good old days are gone; went out in a sort of total eclipse, not yet over, with the advent of the peelers, as the present constables are new termed. Whet number of young bloods, or swells, or mashers, would like to try such jokes on our bobbies— big, strong fellows who can't see a joke, and never could. These are the men responsible for practical jokers over the age of twelve or so becoming rare. You inset with the wags occassionally. Mr. George Gros- smith is one, to was his father, so is Mr. J. L. Toole. In the current month's number of " Casseli's Family Magazine" is an account of an interview with the former. Deploring the fact that he could not play a practical joke on an American during his recent tour, he said he had been more fortunate in his own country,and he went on to say," Yes, I'm rather fond of practical joking. I've often tone, when I've had a friend with me, into some aid City bank—like the Bank of England, for exampled staid place, you know. We've marched up to the counter, and I've said quite calmly to the old gentleman behind it. 'Can't I have a brandy and soda and sonae sandwiches?' They've always been most polite, and taken it seriously, saying. We don't keep brandy and 'soda and sand- wiches here." But you may have them ordered in from outside ? " Yes, but that, of course, is for ourselves,' and so on, all quite gravely and without the suspicion of a smile ; not the slightest quiver of the muscles of the countenance to indicate a sense that a practical joke was fooling around." What a wonderful command of countenance was re- quired by the joker ! But then, that is just what George pa sesses, what he makes his living with. I doubt whether Will Dingley would have succeeded in attempts of that character. He was naturally merry, and his twinkling eye would have betrayed him long before the cleik could have told him about having " sandwiches ordered in from outside." He was " the best of all good company," and ever in request by those who loved a jest even when played at their own expense. It would be tedious to local readers who knew no- thing of the man or the people among whom he lived, if I were to attempt to give a detailed account of the practical jokes he perpetrated. Some, and not the least successful, were made on the spur of the moment. Others were the result of a little consideration, and not a little plotting, and then he hoaxed a mul-e titude. His first great coup, so far as I know, was by trading on the fame of Sam Patch, au American diver or great prowess, who jumped into water from some of the loftiest bridges in the country. One day the walls of Warrington were placarded with announcements that Sam had been in- duced to come to the town on the following afternoon, and had undertaken to dive into the river Mersey from the top of the gable of a very high warehouse belonging to the Old Quay Company, and that a collection would be made on his behalf. Thousands of people went to see the show, they could pay if they liked, not other- wise, and—they were disappointed. The Old Quay officials knew nothing of Sam Patch, and after enduring about an hour's badgering in the way of enquiries, they closed the premises. Not so the crowd, it did not melt away for hours. As the people went away Ding. Icy was delighted to hear them expressing fervent wishee that they could make the acquaintance of the man who had hoaxed them. That was late in the for- ties, to the best of my recollection. I pass over many other tricks, to nanie one that con- sisted of getting a member of the Town Council, a prominent manufacturer, to mount a rocking horse whilst he took the unsuspecting one's photograph. The likeness obtained was reproduced at great cost, and in a remarkable way, and the affair created great amuse- ment, as the sitter was a very eccentric man. Such a joke as that was, however beyond a j )ke. On another occasion the Mayor of the borough, a gentleman whom I will call Mines, a cooper in a large way of business, a worthy citizen fond of a glass and a joke, and one of Dingley's intimates, was made to figure in one of his hoaxes, though hardly as principal. Mr.Mines,lnaay say,died many years ago. At this time the municipal year was drawing towards a close, the elections were over, and the eighth of November had come and almost given place to its successor, the fateful ninth. About half-past eleven o'clock all the doctors in Warrington were waited upon by escited messengers and each told—some having to be called from their beds— "Do make haste ! Please go to Mr. Mines's, for the Mayor is expiring." The worthy practitioners hurried off to his Worship's residence, and were astonished at finding him in the best of health and enjoying supper! Dingley's messengers had omitted to explain that it was the term of office that was expiring. Some of tbe doc- tors accepted his invitation to sit down ; others went off in high dudgeon. Those that remained agreed with Mr. Mines when he said that only Dingley could have con- trived that hoax, for by that time Will had gained a reputation that adhered to him for the rest of his days. Prior to this there was great amusement in the town just after Easter. Wombwell's menagerie had been ex- hibiting at Knott Mill Fair,Manchester, and Warrington being on the road to Liverpool, the next " pitch," it was decided by the management, several days beforehand, to show the ani.nals on the fair ground already men- tioned. Posters adorned the walls, as is usual, on the eve of such visits, and the night before the caravans were to arrive, others in style similar to those already displayed were placarded, saying that Messrs. Womb- well had decided to let the wild man of the woods out of his caravan at Hollins Green, about six miles from Warrington, and that he would walk in cork shoes to the town, and that they most earnestly cautioned the public wt o might desire to wit- ness the sight to keep to the footpaths, as the creature was very ferocious. The proprietors let it be known that after that warning they would not be responsible for anything that occurred in the highway. The announcement caused not a little excitement, particu- larly amongst young people, and an hour before the advertised time of :Lrrival crowds streamed out of the town, going along Manchester-road to meet the mam- moth menagerie. I met Dingley a few days afterwards, and he told me " I never enjoyed myself moro in my lite. I stood opposite the church place (near the end of Man- chester-road), and the people went out in hundreds and hundreds. One man cams to me and said, "This is one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>tO the re 9t of thy tricks, Dinglev," hut he went on with shoe t meet the wild man of the woods walking in ceee I asked him had he 4eaited to see the penPle lejudge whereupon he laughed, and perhaps thought oi s Lynch and his law. To Wombwells the affair "t'tbe fortunate thing. Many people unable to inee„lD menagerie paid visits to it in the fair ground chiL ,'',,rja see their wild but poor relation, and whom not The hoax hoax of 1860 was upon the then rector Teo rington (the Rev. W. Quekett), whom Charles Piero had immortali,ed as " The Medel Curate," and t Lida was I described in the paper mentioned. T rector distant from the centre of the town about three quat lip of a mile. It stands in tine grounds, and WO keP.'cia with the dignity b,fitting the. possession of a comPliatuoi, worth nearly £2,000 a year. Oae day, in with written requests, some apparently from the rec.," and the others from the lady at the head of theio",ba hold,a variety of things were sent up to the rectoryt;fte arrival being timed within an hour. D zees of °eel loads of coal passed along Churce-street, with the 'ads he teri of the minerals being depositeo in the rev. gentllsal cellars ; all the drapers and milliners in the tognAL'i,i' the note sent messengers with boxes of goods, 0 u`,:iesii tailors and grocers, and, to cut it short, so did nea':,',l, the other tradesmen. A hearse or two also aPPear-egi' waggon of timber arrived,and ot barbers to shave 3 ;otl posed sick man there were many, and the Londlre-y1 North-Western Railway Company sent a couple of:400 for the removal of the rector's furniture. Long n all these could arrive, crowds of people assernDle' for watch the fun,as they called it ; nut it was no furl, tie the victims,as may he imagined. Church-streettnea; be end of which the rectory is situated,was some tini.,0,51 fore it resumed its usual appearance. This 1305$ stupid one,it must be confessed. It was strange,—,l6, passion for practical joking got possession of DA'n'h.eir He was a man of parts, and had he chosen Cul" "TO gained a much higher position than he attainod. or failing of his was made amends for in the eyes ringtonians by his goodness of heart and generosecci disposition, and as position in life is not the true Patbo human effort,it may be that Will had, shall I call it'le best of the bargain. Who can say</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS HEROINE. f. The current number of the " Strand Maga-ober; contains an article on " Heroes of the A`noll Medal," and included among t.:.e rest is lady recipient of the medal, Mrs. James Parr, as,'.o ant mistress at the National Schools, St. fieili`glt The circumstances of the courageous deed tilr°o3) which Mrs. Parr (then Miss Hannah Roslothoetr gained the modal are as follows:—On the 140 bloot ber, 1881. the stone belfry of the ,chool Was down during a terrific gale of wind,and teloool the roof into the infants' schoolrooni. where 200 children were assemble.!, causing the dee 10 one, and injuring many others. Whilst others of for safety, MisP Ro.shotham, who vo teaching in another part of the sco, deliberately went in among the falling 01 and clouds of dust, and, while fully consei, of the extreme danger to which she was e%11,00 remained on the spot until every child ha4oo placed in safety. At the imminent riA of he', r life,this heroine of twenty-three removed 0111,0 fants who were partly covrr-41 with the debris',oo' also rescued therefrom a little girl who v7O ;060 pletely buried, and who must eventually have, d suffocated had not such gallantry been dieltsif Representations were made to the proper qui g and Miss Roebotham*.s heroism was recoglaj'ejarr her being made the first and so far only ferias- cipient of an Albert Medal.</p>
